(window.UPANISHAD_DATA=window.UPANISHAD_DATA||{})["nadabindu"]={"slug":"nadabindu","name":"Nādabindu","deva":"नादबिन्दु","note":"","intro":["The Nāda Bindu Upaniṣad (नादबिन्दूपनिषत्, 'Drop of [Inner] Sound') is a minor Upaniṣad of 56 verses, traditionally listed as the 38th among the 108 Upaniṣads. It is one of the twenty Yoga Upaniṣads and one of the five Bindu Upaniṣads (Amṛta-, Amṛtanāda-, Nāda-, Dhyāna-, and Brahma-Bindu). In the standard South-Indian recension followed here it is attached to the Ṛgveda; a North-Indian variant attaches it to the Atharvaveda.","The text is the locus classicus of nāda-anusandhāna — the contemplation of the inner sound — and is the principal scriptural source for the laya-yoga / nāda-yoga stream that flowers later in the Haṭha-Yoga-Pradīpikā (which cites this text extensively in its Caturtha Upadeśa) and in the Goraksha-Paddhati."],"sections":[{"t":"Introduction","u":[{"ref":"1. About the Nāda Bindu Upaniṣad","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Nāda Bindu Upaniṣad (नादबिन्दूपनिषत्, 'Drop of [Inner] Sound') is a minor Upaniṣad of 56 verses, traditionally listed as the 38th among the 108 Upaniṣads. It is one of the twenty Yoga Upaniṣads and one of the five Bindu Upaniṣads (Amṛta-, Amṛtanāda-, Nāda-, Dhyāna-, and Brahma-Bindu). In the standard South-Indian recension followed here it is attached to the Ṛgveda; a North-Indian variant attaches it to the Atharvaveda.","The text is the locus classicus of nāda-anusandhāna — the contemplation of the inner sound — and is the principal scriptural source for the laya-yoga / nāda-yoga stream that flowers later in the Haṭha-Yoga-Pradīpikā (which cites this text extensively in its Caturtha Upadeśa) and in the Goraksha-Paddhati.","Distinctive features of the text:","Two clean sections joined at v. 30: Section I is praṇava-vidyā / haṃsa-vidyā (vv. 1–29); Section II is nāda-anusandhāna proper (vv. 30–56).","Oṃ is anatomised as the cosmic Haṃsa-bird — A is the right wing, U the left, M the tail, and the half-mātrā the head (vv. 1–5).","Four mātrās named Āgneyī, Vāyavyā, Sūryā(Bhānumaṇḍalā), and Vāruṇī (=ardhamātrā) — vv. 6–8.","Twelve kalās (named Ghoṣiṇī, Vidyunmālī, Pataṅgiṇī, Vāyuvegiṇī, Nāmadheyā, Aindrī, Vaiṣṇavī, Śāṅkarī, Mahatī, Dhṛti, Nārī, Brāhmī) — vv. 9–11. Vv. 12–16 then map which kalā at death attains which loka.","A self-contained Advaita interlude on prārabdha-karma using the famous rajju-sarpa (rope-snake) simile (vv. 21–29).","The classical 10 inner nādas — jaladhi, jīmūta, bherī, nirjhara, mardala, ghaṇṭā, kāhala, kiṅkiṇī, vaṃśa-vīṇā, bhramara (vv. 34–35) — repeated by every later nāda-yoga text.","The bhṛṅga-makaranda (bee-in-honey) simile for the mind absorbed in nāda (vv. 42–45) — a signature of this Upaniṣad.","Closing upajāti (v. 56) describing the perfected yogi: gaze fixed without an object, breath still without effort, mind steady without support — 'he is the Brahma-Tāra inner-Nāda form.'"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"2. The Two Major Sections","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Despite the unified title, the text is plainly two compositions in one frame:","Section I (vv. 1–29) — the Praṇava-vidyā. Centred on Oṃ as Haṃsa, the mātrās, the twelve kalās, and (vv. 21–29) a compact Advaita treatment of prārabdha-karma with the rope-snake simile. This section is closest to the other Bindu Upaniṣads (especially Brahma-Bindu and Amṛta-Bindu).","Section II (vv. 30–56) — the Nāda-anusandhāna. The yogi seated in siddhāsana with Vaiṣṇavī-mudrā listens at the right ear (v. 31); first the gross sounds (jaladhi-jīmūta-bherī…), then progressively subtler (kiṅkiṇī-vaṃśa-vīṇā-bhramara) until mind dissolves into cidākāśa (v. 39). This is the technical core of laya-yoga.","Section II is what Haṭha-Yoga-Pradīpikā 4.65–102 quotes and expands. Most readers come to this Upaniṣad for Section II; Section I is the metaphysical setting that makes the practice intelligible."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"3. Manuscript Variations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Two principal recensions, distinguished mainly by Vedic affiliation and minor verse-numbering:","<table><tr><td>Edition</td><td>Verses</td><td>Veda</td><td>Notes</td></tr><tr><td>T.R.S. Ayyangar (Adyar, 1938)</td><td>56</td><td>Ṛgveda</td><td>Standard South-Indian recension; basis of this volume.</td></tr><tr><td>Sanskritdocuments.org</td><td>56</td><td>Ṛgveda</td><td>Same recension, Hattangadi transliteration.</td></tr><tr><td>K. Nārāyaṇaswāmi Aiyar (Thirty Minor Upanishads)</td><td>56</td><td>Ṛgveda</td><td>Early English translation (1914); essentially identical text.</td></tr><tr><td>North-Indian (Atharva) recension</td><td>~50</td><td>Atharvaveda</td><td>Drops or compresses the prārabdha section (vv. 21–29) in some MSS.</td></tr><tr><td>Brahma-Yogin recension</td><td>56</td><td>Ṛgveda</td><td>Basis of Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin's Sanskrit bhāṣya.</td></tr></table>","Where readings affect sense the variant is flagged under the relevant verse. The peace mantra is the Aitareya / Maṇḍūkī 'Oṃ vāṅ me manasi…' — the Ṛgveda-specific śānti."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"4. The Five Commentaries","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The same five commentary lines used for the Amṛta-Bindu Upaniṣad are followed here, so the Bindu-series volumes share a consistent interpretive frame.","Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin (early 18th c., Kāñcī) — the only surviving classical Sanskrit bhāṣya on the 108 minor Upaniṣads. For Nāda-Bindu his commentary is unusually rich on Section II, treating each inner sound as a stage in the gradual dissolution of citta-vṛtti.","Swami Sivananda (Divine Life Society) — for him the Nāda Bindu is THE text on nāda-yoga; his Music as Yoga, Kundalini Yoga, and the practical chapters of Yoga in Daily Life all build on Section II. He distinguishes ten classes of inner sound and reads the bee-in-honey simile (vv. 42–45) as the heart of the practice.","Swami Krishnananda (Divine Life Society) — emphasises the integration of Section I (the metaphysics) and Section II (the technique); reads the loka-sequence (vv. 12–16) as a teaching device, not a doctrine of post-mortem destinations.","T.R.S. Ayyangar (The Yoga Upaniṣads, Adyar, 1938) — the standard English translation; follows Brahma-Yogin and provides essential footnotes on the kalā-names and the loka-sequence.","Paul Deussen (Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda, 1897; Eng. trans. Bedekar/Palsule, Motilal 1980) — Indological reading; locates the text in the Yoga-Upaniṣad layer (later than the principal Upaniṣads), treats vv. 1–20 as the proto-text and vv. 21–29 (the rope-snake passage) as a later Advaitic interpolation that prepares the laya-yoga turn at v. 30.","Note on usage: commentary summaries are paraphrases distilled from the cited published works, not verbatim translations. For citation-grade text consult the primary editions listed in Part V."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Peace Invocation (Śānti-pāṭha)","title":"","sa":["ॐ वाङ्‌मे मनसि प्रतिष्ठिता । मनो मे वाचि प्रतिष्ठितम् ।","आविरावीर्म एधि । वेदस्य म आणीस्थः । श्रुतं मे मा प्रहासीः ।","अनेनाधीतेनाहोरात्रान्संदधामि ।","ऋतं वदिष्यामि । सत्यं वदिष्यामि ।","तन्मामवतु । तद्वक्तारमवतु ।","अवतु मामवतु वक्तारम् ॥","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥"],"iast":["Oṃ vāṅ me manasi pratiṣṭhitā | mano me vāci pratiṣṭhitam | āvirāvīrma edhi | vedasya ma āṇīsthaḥ | śrutaṃ me mā prahāsīḥ | anenādhītenāhorātrān saṃdadhāmi | ṛtaṃ vadiṣyāmi | satyaṃ vadiṣyāmi | tan mām avatu | tad vaktāram avatu | avatu mām avatu vaktāram || Oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ ||"],"words":[],"lit":"May my speech be founded on my mind; may my mind be founded on my speech. O Self-revealing One, reveal Yourself to me. Be the linchpin of the Veda for me. May what I have heard not depart from me. With this my study I shall unite day and night. I shall speak the Right (ṛta); I shall speak the True (satya). May That protect me; may That protect the teacher — protect me, protect the teacher. Oṃ, peace, peace, peace.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This is the Aitareya / Maṇḍūkī śānti-mantra of the Ṛgveda — the appropriate peace invocation for a Ṛgvedic Upaniṣad."]}]},{"t":"","u":[{"ref":"Overture verse","title":"","sa":["वैराजात्मोपासनया सञ्जातज्ञानवह्निना ।","दग्ध्वा कर्मत्रयं योगी यत्पदं याति तद्भजे ॥"],"iast":["vairājātmopāsanayā sañjāta-jñāna-vahninā |","dagdhvā karma-trayaṃ yogī yat-padaṃ yāti tad bhaje ||"],"words":[],"lit":"“By worship of the Vairāja-Ātman, with the fire of knowledge thus kindled, the yogi burns the three karmas (sañcita, prārabdha, āgāmin) and goes to the supreme abode — that I adore.”  (Maṅgalācaraṇa, prefixed to the body of the text.)","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 1","title":"Oṃ as Haṃsa — wings, tail, and head","sa":["ॐ अकारो दक्षिणः पक्ष उकारस्तूत्तरः स्मृतः ।","मकारं पुच्छमित्याहुरर्धमात्रा तु मस्तकम् ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["oṃ akāro dakṣiṇaḥ pakṣa ukāras tūttaraḥ smṛtaḥ |","makāraṃ pucchamityāhur ardhamātrā tu mastakam || 1 ||"],"words":[["a-kāraḥ","m. nom. sg.","(the) syllable A"],["dakṣiṇaḥ pakṣaḥ","m. nom. sg.","right wing"],["u-kāraḥ","m. nom. sg.","(the) syllable U"],["tu uttaraḥ smṛtaḥ","particle + adj. + pp.","indeed (is) declared the left/upper"],["ma-kāram puccham iti","n. acc. + n. acc. + quote","the syllable M, as 'tail'"],["āhuḥ","3 pl. pf. of ah","(they) say"],["ardha-mātrā","f. nom. sg.","the half-mātrā"],["tu mastakam","particle + n. nom.","indeed the head"]],"lit":"“The syllable A is the right wing, U is declared the left/upper (wing); M they call the tail, and the half-mātrā the head.”","para":"Praṇava (Oṃ) is anatomised as the cosmic Haṃsa-bird: A = right wing, U = left wing, M = tail, the silent half-mātrā = head. The four phonetic constituents become a single living form.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the Haṃsa is haṃ-saḥ ('I am That'); Oṃ-as-Haṃsa is the macro-Self viewed through its own self-utterance. The four parts correspond to viśva-taijasa-prājña-turīya of Māṇḍūkya 8–12."],["Sivananda","treats the haṃsa-image as a meditation diagram — visualise the praṇava as a swan, with the half-mātrā (silence) as its 'head' that one steadily approaches."],["Krishnananda","the macrocosmic Haṃsa is also Hiraṇyagarbha; the verse begins a 5-verse construction of the Cosmic Person out of Oṃ-anatomy."],["Ayyangar","'A is the right wing, U the left, M the tail, and the half-mātrā the head' — straight rendering; footnotes the parallel in Haṃsa Upaniṣad."],["Deussen","his Vier-Teiliger-Praṇava section opens here; parallels Māṇḍūkya 8–12 explicit."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 2","title":"Feet, body, eyes — the cosmic anatomy of Oṃ","sa":["पादादिकं गुणास्तस्य शरीरं तत्त्वमुच्यते ।","धर्मोऽस्य दक्षिणश्चक्षुरधर्मो योऽपरः स्मृतः ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["pādādikaṃ guṇās tasya śarīraṃ tattvam ucyate |","dharmo 'sya dakṣiṇaś cakṣur adharmo yo 'paraḥ smṛtaḥ || 2 ||"],"words":[["pāda-ādikam","n. nom. sg. kc.","feet etc."],["guṇāḥ tasya","m. nom. pl. + gen.","(are) its qualities (sattva-rajas-tamas)"],["śarīram tattvam ucyate","n. + n. + pass.","the body is said (to be) tattva"],["dharmaḥ asya dakṣiṇaḥ cakṣuḥ","m. + gen. + adj. + n.","dharma is its right eye"],["adharmaḥ yaḥ aparaḥ smṛtaḥ","m. + rel. + adj. + pp.","adharma is (declared) the other (left)"]],"lit":"“Its feet etc. (limbs) are the guṇas; its body is said (to be) tattva. Its right eye is dharma; the other (eye) is declared (to be) adharma.”","para":"The Haṃsa-Oṃ's anatomy is completed: limbs = sattva-rajas-tamas; body-trunk = the basic tattva-substance; right eye = dharma, left eye = adharma. The cosmic Person of Oṃ is a moral-ontological diagram, not a literal bird.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the body of Oṃ is the (pañca-bhautika) tattva-aggregate; eyes of dharma and adharma are the two pravṛtti-nivṛtti axes of the cosmic Person."],["Sivananda","use the imagery as a mnemonic for the eight 'axes' of Oṃ-anatomy in v. 1–4; don't reify."],["Krishnananda","the right eye / left eye polarity is not moral dualism but the dvandva-axis on which the Cosmic Person looks at its own creation."],["Ayyangar","renders cleanly; footnotes the parallel in Mahānārāyaṇa on the cosmic Puruṣa-anatomy."],["Deussen","compares Aitareya 1.1.4 on the gradual unfolding of the cosmic Puruṣa."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 3","title":"Lokas in the body — feet to navel","sa":["भूर्लोकः पादयोस्तस्य भुवर्लोकस्तु जानुनि ।","सुवर्लोकः कटीदेशे नाभिदेशे महर्जगत् ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["bhūrlokaḥ pādayos tasya bhuvarlokas tu jānuni |","suvarlokaḥ kaṭīdeśe nābhideśe maharjagat || 3 ||"],"words":[["bhūḥ-lokaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Bhūr-loka (earth-realm)"],["pādayoḥ tasya","m. loc. du. + gen.","in its (two) feet"],["bhuvaḥ-lokaḥ tu jānuni","m. nom. + particle + loc.","Bhuvar-loka indeed in the knees"],["suvaḥ-lokaḥ kaṭī-deśe","m. + n. loc. sg.","Svar-loka in the waist-region"],["nābhi-deśe","n. loc. sg.","in the navel-region"],["mahaḥ-jagat","n. nom. sg.","the Mahar-world"]],"lit":"“Bhūr-loka is in its (two) feet; Bhuvar-loka is in the knees; Svar-loka in the waist; in the navel-region is the Mahar-world.”","para":"The seven lokas are mapped onto the body of the Haṃsa-Oṃ from below upward: Bhūr (feet) → Bhuvar (knees) → Svar (waist) → Mahar (navel). The remaining three lokas follow in v. 4.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the loka-body identification is a tantrik-yogic transposition of the Vyāhṛti-formula — the same lokas the daily Sandhya-arghya invokes are now read off the yogi's own body."],["Sivananda","useful in prāṇāyāma — locate the breath in each loka-region during retention; the whole text is to be visualised inside the body of the haṃsa-praṇava."],["Krishnananda","the verse subverts the cosmological map by placing it inside; lokas are not 'out there' but axes of the cosmic Person."],["Ayyangar","annotates the seven Vyāhṛtis (Bhūr-Bhuvar-Suvar-Mahar-Janar-Tapar-Satya); compares the daily-sandhya formula."],["Deussen","compares the body-loka mapping in Mahānārāyaṇa 10 and Maitri 6.6."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 4","title":"Lokas in the body — heart to forehead","sa":["जनोलोकस्तु हृद्देशे कण्ठे लोकस्तपस्ततः ।","भ्रुवोर्ललाटमध्ये तु सत्यलोको व्यवस्थितः ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["janoloka stu hṛddeśe kaṇṭhe lokas tapas tataḥ |","bhruvor lalāṭa-madhye tu satyaloko vyavasthitaḥ || 4 ||"],"words":[["jano-lokaḥ tu hṛd-deśe","m. nom. + n. loc.","Janar-loka indeed in the heart-region"],["kaṇṭhe lokaḥ tapaḥ tataḥ","m. loc. + m. nom. + adv.","in the throat (is) the Tapar-loka (lit. 'the world Tapas') next"],["bhruvoḥ lalāṭa-madhye","f. gen. du. + n. loc.","between the eyebrows, in the forehead-middle"],["satya-lokaḥ vyavasthitaḥ","m. nom. + pp.","Satya-loka (is) established"]],"lit":"“Janar-loka is in the heart-region; in the throat the Tapar-loka; between the eyebrows, in the forehead-middle, is established Satya-loka.”","para":"Completion of the loka-body map: Janar (heart) → Tapar (throat) → Satya (between brows / forehead). The Cosmic Person of Oṃ now contains all seven worlds.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","Satya-loka in the forehead-middle = ājñā-cakra in later tantric terms; the verse anticipates Section II's location of nāda-listening at the trikūṭa region."],["Sivananda","use the seven body-loka pairings as anchors during prāṇāyāma — the loka of Satya is the focus of dhyāna in this Upaniṣad's practice."],["Krishnananda","vyavasthitaḥ is striking — Satya-loka is not visited; it is 'set up' (vyavasthita) in the forehead — i.e. it has its standing-place there."],["Ayyangar","comments on the unusual ordering of Tapaḥ-loka before Satya-loka in some traditions."],["Deussen","compares Yoga-Tattva 86–87 for the same loka-body pairing."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 5","title":"The Haṃsa-yogin breaks all karma","sa":["सहस्रार्णमतीवात्र मन्त्र एष प्रदर्शितः ।","एवमेतां समारूढो हंसयोगविचक्षणः ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["sahasrārṇam atīvātra mantra eṣa pradarśitaḥ |","evam etāṃ samārūḍho haṃsayoga-vicakṣaṇaḥ || 5 ||"],"words":[["sahasra-arṇam atīva atra","n. acc. + adv. + adv.","(with) a thousand syllables, exceedingly, here"],["mantraḥ eṣaḥ pradarśitaḥ","m. nom. + pron. + pp.","this mantra (has been) revealed"],["evam etām samārūḍhaḥ","adv. + f. acc. + pp.","having thus mounted this (Haṃsa)"],["haṃsa-yoga-vicakṣaṇaḥ","m. nom. sg. bahuvr.","(the one) skilled-in-Haṃsa-yoga"]],"lit":"“With a thousand syllables — exceedingly so — this mantra has here been revealed. Having thus mounted this (Haṃsa), the one skilled in Haṃsa-yoga…” (verse continues into v. 6)","para":"This Oṃ-as-Haṃsa is a mantra of a 'thousand syllables' (i.e. inexhaustible). One who climbs this Haṃsa-yoga — proceeds to v. 6's conclusion.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","sahasrārṇam is hyperbolic — the praṇava holds in nucleus what would otherwise need a thousand-syllabled exposition."],["Sivananda","samārūḍhaḥ is the meditator-on-the-haṃsa; the mantra is 'mounted' as a vehicle to the higher state."],["Krishnananda","haṃsa-yoga = ham-sa-yoga = ajapā-japa of the natural breath; the mounting is not aspirational but the recognition of what already happens with every breath."],["Ayyangar","renders sahasrārṇam as 'the thousand-syllabled' — the Vedic name for the inexhaustible praṇava."],["Deussen","compares 'sahasram aśva' usage in RV 1.164 — the inexhaustibility-trope."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 6","title":"… is not destroyed by crores of sins · First mātrā: Āgneyī","sa":["न भिद्यते कर्मचारैः पापकोटिशतैरपि ।","आग्नेयी प्रथमा मात्रा वायव्येषा तथापरा ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["na bhidyate karmacāraiḥ pāpa-koṭi-śatair api |","āgneyī prathamā mātrā vāyavyeṣā tathāparā || 6 ||"],"words":[["na bhidyate","3 sg. pres. pass.","is not broken / destroyed"],["karma-cāraiḥ","m. instr. pl.","by karma-courses / strands of karma"],["pāpa-koṭi-śataiḥ api","n. instr. pl. + emph.","even by hundreds of crores of sins"],["āgneyī prathamā mātrā","f. nom. sg.","the first mātrā (is) Āgneyī (fire-natured, = A)"],["vāyavī eṣā tathā aparā","f. nom. + pron. + adv. + adj.","this other (is) likewise Vāyavī (wind-natured, = U)"]],"lit":"“(He) is not destroyed by strands of karma, even by hundreds of crores of sins. The first mātrā (of Oṃ) is Āgneyī, this other (next) is likewise Vāyavī.”","para":"The Haṃsa-yogi cannot be undone by any amount of bad karma. Now the four mātrās of Oṃ are introduced: A = fiery (Āgneyī), U = airy (Vāyavī).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the first hemistich completes v. 5; the second begins a new technical exposition (mātrās). The break between content units (yogi-mokṣa / mātrā-analysis) inside a single śloka is not unusual in the Upaniṣads."],["Sivananda","the four mātrās correspond to four elemental qualities: fire-air-sun-water (the last is the ardha-mātrā). The yogi is to feel each quality during pronunciation."],["Krishnananda","the elemental coding is upacāra — A is 'fiery' because outgoing, U is 'airy' because moving; the labels are not literal element-identities."],["Ayyangar","footnotes karma-cāraiḥ as 'currents of karma'."],["Deussen","his §1 of Vier-Teiliger-Praṇava-Lehre: A=Agni, U=Vāyu — older mātrā coding."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 7","title":"Third and fourth mātrās — Bhānumaṇḍala and Vāruṇī","sa":["भानुमण्डलसङ्काशा भवेन्मात्रा तथोत्तरा ।","परमा चार्धमात्रा या वारुणीं तां विदुर्बुधाः ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["bhānumaṇḍala-saṅkāśā bhaven mātrā tathottarā |","paramā cārdhamātrā yā vāruṇīṃ tāṃ vidur budhāḥ || 7 ||"],"words":[["bhānu-maṇḍala-saṅkāśā","f. nom. sg. kc.","resembling the sun's disc"],["bhavet mātrā tathā uttarā","3 sg. pot. + f. + adv. + adj.","(would) be the mātrā, likewise the next (= M)"],["paramā ca ardha-mātrā yā","adj. + conj. + f. + rel.","and the supreme half-mātrā which (is)"],["vāruṇīm tām viduḥ budhāḥ","f. acc. + pron. + 3 pl. pf. + nom.","the wise know (it as) Vāruṇī"]],"lit":"“The third mātrā would be like the disc of the sun (Bhānumaṇḍala-saṅkāśā); and the supreme half-mātrā, the wise know that (to be) Vāruṇī.”","para":"M is sun-disc-like (Bhānumaṇḍala-saṅkāśā / Sūryamaṇḍalā); the half-mātrā (silent residue) is Vāruṇī (water-natured). The four mātrās map to the four elements Agni-Vāyu-Sūrya-Varuṇa, ascending from gross to subtle.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the half-mātrā is Vāruṇī because water is the most subtle of the gross elements; what comes after it (silence) is beyond elements altogether."],["Sivananda","during the M-prolongation the practitioner visualises the sun-disc; during the post-M silence (ardha-mātrā) the felt-quality is cool, water-like."],["Krishnananda","the four labels are mnemonics; the real point is the graded refinement A→U→M→half-mātrā → silence."],["Ayyangar","renders Vāruṇī as 'of Varuṇa' — the deity of waters and of the cosmic ṛta; resonant with the peace-mantra's 'ṛtaṃ vadiṣyāmi'."],["Deussen","third mātrā = Sūrya, fourth (ardha) = Varuṇa — same elemental ordering as Yoga-Cūḍāmaṇi."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 8","title":"These mātrās stand in the three times — hear of the dhāraṇās","sa":["कालत्रयेऽपि यत्रेमा मात्रा नूनं प्रतिष्ठिताः ।","एष ओङ्कार आख्यातो धारणाभिर्निबोधत ॥ ८ ॥"],"iast":["kālatraye 'pi yatremā mātrā nūnaṃ pratiṣṭhitāḥ |","eṣa oṅkāra ākhyāto dhāraṇābhir nibodhata || 8 ||"],"words":[["kāla-traye api","n. loc. sg. + emph.","in the three times also"],["yatra imāḥ mātrāḥ","rel. adv. + f. nom. pl.","wherein these mātrās"],["nūnam pratiṣṭhitāḥ","adv. + f. nom. pl. pp.","verily stand established"],["eṣaḥ oṅkāraḥ ākhyātaḥ","m. nom. sg.","this Oṃ-syllable has been declared"],["dhāraṇābhiḥ nibodhata","f. instr. pl. + 2 pl. impv.","(through) the dhāraṇās, learn!"]],"lit":"“In the three times (past, present, future) wherein these mātrās verily stand established — this Oṃ has been declared; now learn (of it) through the dhāraṇās.”","para":"The four mātrās are stable across all three times — they are not merely sound-events but timeless. The four mātrās become the basis for the twelve dhāraṇās / kalās enumerated in vv. 9–11.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","kāla-traya = past/present/future; the mātrās 'stand' in all three because they are not phonemes but the very fabric of the trikāla-Brahman."],["Sivananda","the practical hint is that each of the four mātrās subdivides into three (past-present-future) → 12 kalās = sub-mātrās. Vv. 9–11 then name them."],["Krishnananda","kāla-traya here also means the three dhātus / liṅgas of Vedic chanting (udātta, anudātta, svarita); 4 mātrās × 3 = 12 kalās."],["Ayyangar","explicitly: 'the three svaras — udātta, anudātta, svarita' produce the twelve kalās from the four mātrās."],["Deussen","follows the udātta-anudātta-svarita reading — the 12 kalās are produced by tone-modulations of the 4 mātrās."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 9","title":"The Twelve Kalās — first four","sa":["घोषिणी प्रथमा मात्रा विद्युन्मात्रा तथाऽपरा ।","पतङ्गिनी तृतीया स्याच्चतुर्थी वायुवेगिनी ॥ ९ ॥"],"iast":["ghoṣiṇī prathamā mātrā vidyunmātrā tathāparā |","pataṅginī tṛtīyā syāc caturthī vāyuveginī || 9 ||"],"words":[["ghoṣiṇī prathamā mātrā","f. nom. sg.","Ghoṣiṇī (the resonating) is the first kalā"],["vidyut-mātrā tathā aparā","f. nom. + adv. + adj.","Vidyunmātrā (the lightning-measure) likewise the next"],["pataṅginī tṛtīyā syāt","f. nom. + adj. + pot.","Pataṅginī (the moth/flying-one) would be the third"],["caturthī vāyu-veginī","adj. + f. nom. bahuvr.","the fourth (is) Vāyuveginī (wind-speeded)"]],"lit":"“Ghoṣiṇī is the first kalā, Vidyunmātrā likewise the next; Pataṅginī would be the third, the fourth Vāyuveginī.”","para":"First four of the twelve kalās: 1) Ghoṣiṇī (the resounding one), 2) Vidyunmātrā / Vidyunmālī (the lightning-garlanded), 3) Pataṅginī (the flying / moth-like), 4) Vāyuveginī (wind-speed).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the kalā-names are descriptive sound-qualities — what the praṇava 'feels like' at each stage of subtilisation. Ghoṣiṇī is a loud audible reverberation; Vāyuveginī is the swift airy stage."],["Sivananda","the kalā-list is to be memorised by the nāda-yogi; one learns to recognise which stage one's japa has reached by the felt quality of the sound."],["Krishnananda","the names are deva-laden in vv. 10–11 (Aindrī, Vaiṣṇavī, Śāṅkarī, Brāhmī) — the praṇava modulates through the deity-currents of the Vedic pantheon."],["Ayyangar","footnotes the alternate reading Vidyunmālī (lightning-garlanded) for Vidyunmātrā in some MSS."],["Deussen","the names parallel the Cāmuṇḍā / Devī kalā-lists in later Tantra (e.g. Lalitā-Sahasranāma's 16 kalās of the moon)."],["Textual note","vidyunmAtrA (sanskritdocuments.org reading) ↔ vidyunmAlA / vidyunmAlinI in Ayyangar and some Adyar MSS. We follow the SD reading."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 10","title":"The Twelve Kalās — fifth to eighth","sa":["पञ्चमी नामधेया तु षष्ठी चैन्द्र्यभिधीयते ।","सप्तमी वैष्णवी नाम अष्टमी शाङ्करीति च ॥ १० ॥"],"iast":["pañcamī nāmadheyā tu ṣaṣṭhī caindryabhidhīyate |","saptamī vaiṣṇavī nāma aṣṭamī śāṅkarīti ca || 10 ||"],"words":[["pañcamī nāmadheyā tu","f. nom. + adj. + particle","the fifth (is called) Nāmadheyā"],["ṣaṣṭhī ca aindrī abhidhīyate","adj. + conj. + f. + pass.","and the sixth is called Aindrī (Indra's)"],["saptamī vaiṣṇavī nāma","f. nom. + adj. + indecl.","the seventh (is) named Vaiṣṇavī (Viṣṇu's)"],["aṣṭamī śāṅkarī iti ca","f. nom. + adj. + quote-marker","and the eighth (is) Śāṅkarī (Śaṅkara's / Śiva's)"]],"lit":"“The fifth is called Nāmadheyā; the sixth is called Aindrī; the seventh is named Vaiṣṇavī; and the eighth Śāṅkarī.”","para":"Kalās 5–8: 5) Nāmadheyā (the 'name-giving' or 'so-named'), 6) Aindrī (of Indra), 7) Vaiṣṇavī (of Viṣṇu), 8) Śāṅkarī (of Śaṅkara/Śiva).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","from the fifth kalā onward the praṇava aligns successively with the divinities — Indra (rāja), Viṣṇu (sthiti), Śiva (laya). The vertical climb is also a tri-mūrti ascent."],["Sivananda","use the deity-association as a meditative anchor — at the seventh kalā imagine the form of Viṣṇu, at the eighth that of Śiva."],["Krishnananda","the deities are not separate persons but functional axes of the cosmic process; the kalā-yogi's japa traverses them all."],["Ayyangar","renders aindrī as 'pertaining to Indra' — i.e. the kalā associated with the regent of svarga."],["Deussen","compares the deva-sequence Indra–Viṣṇu–Śiva in Yoga-Cūḍāmaṇi 18–20."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 11","title":"The Twelve Kalās — ninth to twelfth","sa":["नवमी महती नाम धृतिस्तु दशमी मता ।","एकादशी भवेन्नारी ब्राह्मी तु द्वादशी परा ॥ ११ ॥"],"iast":["navamī mahatī nāma dhṛtis tu daśamī matā |","ekādaśī bhaven nārī brāhmī tu dvādaśī parā || 11 ||"],"words":[["navamī mahatī nāma","f. nom. + adj. + indecl.","the ninth (is) named Mahatī (the great)"],["dhṛtiḥ tu daśamī matā","f. nom. + particle + adj. + pp.","Dhṛti (firmness) is held (to be) the tenth"],["ekādaśī bhavet nārī","adj. + 3 sg. pot. + f. nom.","the eleventh would be Nārī (woman / feminine)"],["brāhmī tu dvādaśī parā","f. nom. + particle + adj. + adj.","Brāhmī (Brahman-natured) is the supreme twelfth"]],"lit":"“The ninth is named Mahatī; Dhṛti is held (to be) the tenth; the eleventh would be Nārī; the supreme twelfth (is) Brāhmī.”","para":"Kalās 9–12: 9) Mahatī (the Great), 10) Dhṛti (Firmness), 11) Nārī (the Feminine / 'woman'), 12) Brāhmī (Brahman-natured) — the climax.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the climax Brāhmī is the kalā in which the praṇava ceases to be a sound and becomes Brahman-knowledge; Mahatī, Dhṛti, Nārī are its three preparatory aspects."],["Sivananda","the twelve-kalā ascent is a meditation ladder; the practitioner is not to dwell on the elemental and deity correspondences but to use them as rungs."],["Krishnananda","Brāhmī is the asparśa-yoga end-state of Gauḍapāda — sound at last as silent, indistinguishable from its own substrate."],["Ayyangar","renders Nārī as 'pertaining to Nara (man)' or simply 'feminine'; he notes the rare gendering."],["Deussen","the 12-kalā list is closest to that in Yoga-Cūḍāmaṇi and may share a source with that text."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 12","title":"Death in the first kalā — universal sovereign in Bhārata","sa":["प्रथमायां तु मात्रायां यदि प्राणैर्वियुज्यते ।","भरते वर्षराजासौ सार्वभौमः प्रजायते ॥ १२ ॥"],"iast":["prathamāyāṃ tu mātrāyāṃ yadi prāṇair viyujyate |","bharate varṣarājāsau sārvabhaumaḥ prajāyate || 12 ||"],"words":[["prathamāyām tu mātrāyām","f. loc. sg. + particle","in the first mātrā / kalā indeed"],["yadi prāṇaiḥ viyujyate","conj. + m. instr. pl. + 3 sg. pres. pass.","if (one) is separated from the prāṇas"],["bharate varṣa-rāja asau","n. loc. sg. + m. nom. sg.","in Bhārata-varṣa, this man (becomes) the king"],["sārvabhaumaḥ prajāyate","m. nom. + 3 sg. pres.","(as) a universal sovereign is born"]],"lit":"“If, in the first mātrā / kalā, one is separated from the prāṇas (= dies), one is born in Bhārata-varṣa as a universal sovereign (sārvabhauma).”","para":"Now begins the loka-/birth-sequence (vv. 12–16): leaving the body during meditation on the n-th kalā determines the next birth. First kalā → universal emperor of Bhārata.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","treats the sequence as a teaching of graded fruits — not literal post-mortem cartography but a way of saying 'the higher the kalā at death, the higher the fruit'. The point is that the highest kalā (Brāhmī, v. 16) yields mokṣa."],["Sivananda","reads this as a motivation-text: even short of mokṣa, every degree of progress in praṇava-japa has a positive after-fruit."],["Krishnananda","the loka-sequence is a karma-phala teaching wrapped in yogic terms — not a literal prophecy. Its real force is the ascending order, climaxing in v. 16."],["Ayyangar","renders sārvabhauma precisely as 'cakravartin'; cites Mahābhārata parallels."],["Deussen","compares the parallel in Yoga-Cūḍāmaṇi where the same death-during-mātrā sequence yields lokas — likely a shared upper-Yoga-Upaniṣad common source."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 13","title":"Second to fourth — Yakṣa, Vidyādhara, Gandharva","sa":["द्वितीयायां समुत्क्रान्तो भवेद्यक्षो महात्मवान् ।","विद्याधरस्तृतीयायां गान्धर्वस्तु चतुर्थिका ॥ १३ ॥"],"iast":["dvitīyāyāṃ samutkrānto bhaved yakṣo mahātmavān |","vidyādharas tṛtīyāyāṃ gāndharvas tu caturthikā || 13 ||"],"words":[["dvitīyāyām samutkrāntaḥ","f. loc. + m. nom. pp.","(one) departed (from the body) in the second"],["bhavet yakṣaḥ mahā-ātmavān","3 sg. pot. + m. nom. + adj.","would be a great-souled Yakṣa"],["vidyādharaḥ tṛtīyāyām","m. nom. + f. loc.","(in the) third (becomes) a Vidyādhara"],["gāndharvaḥ tu caturthikā","m. nom. + particle + f. nom.","a Gandharva (in the) fourth"]],"lit":"“In the second (kalā), having departed, one would be a great-souled Yakṣa; in the third, a Vidyādhara; in the fourth, a Gandharva.”","para":"Kalā 2 → Yakṣa (semi-divine guardian); 3 → Vidyādhara (sky-faring wisdom-bearer); 4 → Gandharva (celestial musician).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the ascending order Yakṣa → Vidyādhara → Gandharva tracks Vedic-Purāṇic semi-divine hierarchies; each is a step closer to the celestial worlds."],["Sivananda","useful for visualisation; not to be taken as a literal life-plan, but as an index of the increasing 'lightness' of consciousness with each higher kalā."],["Krishnananda","Yakṣa-Vidyādhara-Gandharva = three steps of the antarikṣa-domain; the fifth and onward go to the deva-lokas (v. 14)."],["Ayyangar","annotates the three semi-divine classes in detail; cites their habitats from the Mahābhārata."],["Deussen","compares Kauṣītaki Upaniṣad 1.4 for similar deva-rūpa-ascent."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 14","title":"Fifth kalā — Soma-loka","sa":["पञ्चम्यामथ मात्रायां यदि प्राणैर्वियुज्यते ।","उषितः सह देवत्वं सोमलोके महीयते ॥ १४ ॥"],"iast":["pañcamyām atha mātrāyāṃ yadi prāṇair viyujyate |","uṣitaḥ saha devatvaṃ somaloke mahīyate || 14 ||"],"words":[["pañcamyām atha mātrāyām","f. loc. + adv. + f. loc.","and in the fifth mātrā"],["yadi prāṇaiḥ viyujyate","conj. + instr. pl. + pres. pass.","if (one) is separated from the prāṇas"],["uṣitaḥ saha devatvam","m. nom. pp. + indecl. + n. acc.","having dwelt, together with divinity"],["soma-loke mahīyate","m. loc. + 3 sg. pres. pass.","is exalted in Soma-loka"]],"lit":"“And if, in the fifth mātrā, one is separated from the prāṇas — having dwelt (there), one is exalted in Soma-loka, together with divinity.”","para":"At the fifth kalā the yogi crosses into deva-territory — Soma-loka (the moon-world), with divine status.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","saha devatvaṃ = with the status of devas; not 'becoming a deva' but 'sharing in their condition'."],["Sivananda","Soma-loka is the cool, blissful world of the moon — the felt-quality of the fifth kalā's japa is similarly cool and luminous."],["Krishnananda","the verse's syntax — uṣitaḥ saha devatvam — is awkward but signals 'having dwelt (a while), goes on with the gods'."],["Ayyangar","Soma-loka here = candra-loka; cf. Chāndogya 5.10.1."],["Deussen","the verse's prosody is irregular; he flags possible textual corruption but the sense is clear."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 15","title":"Sixth–eighth — Indra-sāyujya, Vaiṣṇavī, Rudra-loka","sa":["षष्ठ्यामिन्द्रस्य सायुज्यं सप्तम्यां वैष्णवं पदम् ।","अष्टम्यां व्रजते रुद्रं पशूनां च पतिं तथा ॥ १५ ॥"],"iast":["ṣaṣṭhyām indrasya sāyujyaṃ saptamyāṃ vaiṣṇavaṃ padam |","aṣṭamyāṃ vrajate rudraṃ paśūnāṃ ca patiṃ tathā || 15 ||"],"words":[["ṣaṣṭhyām indrasya sāyujyam","f. loc. + m. gen. + n. nom.","in the sixth, Indra's sāyujya (union with Indra)"],["saptamyām vaiṣṇavam padam","f. loc. + n. nom.","in the seventh, the Viṣṇu-state"],["aṣṭamyām vrajate rudram","f. loc. + 3 sg. pres. + m. acc.","in the eighth, (one) goes to Rudra"],["paśūnām ca patim tathā","m. gen. pl. + conj. + m. acc. + adv.","and to the Lord of Animals (Paśupati) likewise"]],"lit":"“In the sixth (kalā), Indra's sāyujya (union); in the seventh, the Vaiṣṇava state; in the eighth, one goes to Rudra and to Paśupati likewise.”","para":"Kalā 6 → sāyujya with Indra; 7 → Viṣṇu's state; 8 → Rudra / Paśupati. The deva-sequence climbs the trimūrti.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the climb through Indra → Viṣṇu → Rudra parallels the kalā-deity assignments of vv. 9–11 (Aindrī, Vaiṣṇavī, Śāṅkarī); the deities reappear here as destinations."],["Sivananda","sāyujya (union) is the second-highest of the four post-mortem states (sālokya, sāmīpya, sārūpya, sāyujya); the kalā-yogi attains the highest progressively."],["Krishnananda","the parallelism is the verse's whole point: the kalā in which you die is also the deva-cipher of that kalā."],["Ayyangar","renders Rudraṃ paśūnāṃ pati as 'Rudra, the Lord of Animals' — emphasising the older Vedic Rudra-Paśupati identity."],["Deussen","compares Śvetāśvatara 3.5 on Rudra as Paśupati."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 16","title":"Ninth–twelfth — Mahar, Jana, Tapas, eternal Brahman","sa":["नवम्यां तु महर्लोकं दशम्यां तु जनं व्रजेत् ।","एकादश्यां तपोलोकं द्वादश्यां ब्रह्म शाश्वतम् ॥ १६ ॥"],"iast":["navamyāṃ tu maharlokaṃ daśamyāṃ tu janaṃ vrajet |","ekādaśyāṃ tapolokaṃ dvādaśyāṃ brahma śāśvatam || 16 ||"],"words":[["navamyām tu mahaḥ-lokam","f. loc. + n. acc.","in the ninth, the Mahar-loka"],["daśamyām tu janam vrajet","f. loc. + m. acc. + 3 sg. pot.","in the tenth, (one) would go to Janar-loka"],["ekādaśyām tapaḥ-lokam","f. loc. + n. acc.","in the eleventh, the Tapar-loka"],["dvādaśyām brahma śāśvatam","f. loc. + n. acc.","in the twelfth, eternal Brahman"]],"lit":"“In the ninth, one would go to Mahar-loka; in the tenth, to Janar; in the eleventh, to Tapar-loka; in the twelfth, (to) eternal Brahman.”","para":"Climax of the loka-sequence: the twelfth kalā (Brāhmī) is no loka at all but Brahman itself, eternal — the mokṣa-fruit.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","śāśvatam brahma is the goal of the entire kalā-vidyā; everything below it is provisional. Hence vv. 12–15 are not 'destinations' but a graded ladder."],["Sivananda","v. 16 is the goal verse — even the highest of the seven lokas (Satya) is short of the mokṣa promised by Brāhmī-kalā."],["Krishnananda","the verse closes the deva/loka-cartography and pivots to the supreme exposition (vv. 17–20). After v. 16, lokas are no longer in view; Brahman alone."],["Ayyangar","footnotes the parallel between this verse's seven lokas and v. 3–4's body-loka mapping."],["Deussen","marks v. 16 as the climax of Section I's first half (mātrā-/kalā-/loka- section)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 17","title":"The pure, all-pervading, supreme Brahman","sa":["ततः परतरं शुद्धं व्यापकं निर्मलं शिवम् ।","सदोदितं परं ब्रह्म ज्योतिषामुदयो यतः ॥ १७ ॥"],"iast":["tataḥ parataraṃ śuddhaṃ vyāpakaṃ nirmalaṃ śivam |","sadoditaṃ paraṃ brahma jyotiṣām udayo yataḥ || 17 ||"],"words":[["tataḥ parataram","adv. + adj.","higher than that"],["śuddham vyāpakam","n. nom. sg.","pure, all-pervading"],["nirmalam śivam","n. nom. sg.","stainless, auspicious"],["sadā-uditam param brahma","adv. + n. + adj. + n.","ever-risen supreme Brahman"],["jyotiṣām udayaḥ yataḥ","n. gen. pl. + m. nom. + rel.","whence (is) the rising of lights"]],"lit":"“Higher than that — pure, all-pervading, stainless, auspicious — ever-risen, the supreme Brahman, from which (comes) the rising of lights.”","para":"Beyond even the twelfth kalā is the Pure, Pervading, Stainless, Auspicious, Ever-Risen Supreme Brahman — the source from which sun, moon, fire, and all luminous bodies arise.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","sadoditam — 'always risen' — is the via-positiva pair of Māṇḍūkya's via-negativa lakṣaṇas. The supreme is not 'reached' but ever-already arisen."],["Sivananda","use the four positive epithets (śuddha-vyāpaka-nirmala-śiva) as meditation seeds at the highest stage of nāda-japa."],["Krishnananda","jyotiṣām udayaḥ = the rising-source of all light; cf. Muṇḍaka 2.2.10 (tasya bhāsā sarvam idaṃ vibhāti)."],["Ayyangar","renders the verse cleanly; cross-references Muṇḍaka 2.2.10."],["Deussen","compares Śvetāśvatara 6.14 (na tatra sūryo bhāti…)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 18","title":"Transcending the senses — mind dissolves into Śiva","sa":["अतीन्द्रियं गुणातीतं मनो लीनं यदा भवेत् ।","अनूपमं शिवं शान्तं योगयुक्तं सदा विशेत् ॥ १८ ॥"],"iast":["atīndriyaṃ guṇātītaṃ mano līnaṃ yadā bhavet |","anūpamaṃ śivaṃ śāntaṃ yoga-yuktaṃ sadā viśet || 18 ||"],"words":[["atīndriyam guṇa-atītam","n. acc. + adj.","beyond the senses, beyond the guṇas"],["manaḥ līnam yadā bhavet","n. nom. + pp. + correl. + pot.","when the mind becomes dissolved"],["anūpamam śivam śāntam","n. acc.","incomparable, auspicious, peaceful (One)"],["yoga-yuktam sadā viśet","n. acc. + adv. + pot.","(then one) yoked-in-yoga should ever enter"]],"lit":"“When the mind, beyond the senses and beyond the guṇas, becomes dissolved (līnam), then one yoked in yoga should ever enter the incomparable, auspicious, peaceful (Brahman).”","para":"When the mind has crossed the senses and the three guṇas and is dissolved, the yogi enters the unmatched, śiva, śānta supreme.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","mano-laya (dissolution of mind) is here distinguished from mere śūnyatā — the mind is not 'nothing'; it has gone home (līnam) to its source."],["Sivananda","first explicit use of laya in the text — the yoga of Nāda-Bindu is laya-yoga proper."],["Krishnananda","guṇa-atīta is the Gītā's expression (14.20) — the same state, here described as laya not as guṇātītatva."],["Ayyangar","translates yoga-yuktaḥ as 'concentrated in yoga'."],["Deussen","compares Yoga-Cūḍāmaṇi 24 for almost identical phrasing."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 19","title":"Body shed slowly through yogic discipline","sa":["तद्युक्तस्तन्मयो जन्तुः शनैर्मुञ्चेत्कलेवरम् ।","संस्थितो योगचारेण सर्वसङ्गविवर्जितः ॥ १९ ॥"],"iast":["tad-yuktas tan-mayo jantuḥ śanair muñcet kalevaram |","saṃsthito yogacāreṇa sarva-saṅga-vivarjitaḥ || 19 ||"],"words":[["tat-yuktaḥ tat-mayaḥ","m. nom. sg.","yoked to That, made of That"],["jantuḥ","m. nom. sg.","(this) being"],["śanaiḥ muñcet kalevaram","adv. + 3 sg. pot. + n. acc.","would slowly release the body"],["saṃsthitaḥ yoga-cāreṇa","m. nom. + m. instr.","established by yoga-practice"],["sarva-saṅga-vivarjitaḥ","m. nom. sg. bahuvr.","freed from all attachments"]],"lit":"“Being yoked to That and made of That, the (yogi-)being should slowly release the body — established by yoga-practice, freed from all attachment.”","para":"The yogi, identified with the supreme and free of all attachments, gradually lets the body fall away through sustained yoga-practice.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the release is śanair, gradually — not a violent shedding but a natural fading. The verse describes jīvanmukti turning into videhamukti."],["Sivananda","the body's release is involuntary at this stage; the practitioner does not 'leave' the body but ceases to identify with it."],["Krishnananda","tad-yuktas tan-mayaḥ = the double formula: first yoked, then made-of; identification stages are progressive."],["Ayyangar","renders śanaiḥ muñcet as 'gradually relinquishes' — emphasising the natural-decay character."],["Deussen","compares the Bhagavad-Gītā 5.21–22 process-of-letting-go formulations."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 20","title":"Bonds dissolved, the yogi tastes supreme bliss","sa":["ततो विलीनपाशोऽसौ विमलः कमलाप्रभुः ।","तेनैव ब्रह्मभावेन परमानन्दमश्नुते ॥ २० ॥"],"iast":["tato vilīna-pāśo 'sau vimalaḥ kamalāprabhuḥ |","tenaiva brahma-bhāvena paramānandam aśnute || 20 ||"],"words":[["tataḥ vilīna-pāśaḥ asau","adv. + m. nom. pp. + pron.","then, this one, whose bonds are dissolved"],["vimalaḥ kamalā-prabhuḥ","m. nom. + m. nom.","stainless, Lord-of-Kamalā (Lakṣmī's Lord, i.e. Viṣṇu)"],["tena eva brahma-bhāvena","m. instr.","by that very Brahman-state"],["parama-ānandam aśnute","m. acc. + 3 sg. pres.","enjoys supreme bliss"]],"lit":"“Then this (yogi), whose bonds are dissolved, stainless, Lord of Kamalā (= identified with Viṣṇu), enjoys supreme bliss by that very Brahman-state.”","para":"With all bonds gone, the stainless one becomes Kamalā-prabhu (the Lord of Lakṣmī, i.e. Nārāyaṇa Himself) and tastes paramānanda in that Brahman-state.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","kamalā-prabhuḥ is Vāsudeva — same closing identification we saw in Amṛta-Bindu v. 22. The Bindu-Upaniṣad family consistently closes Section I with a sectarian-flavoured but Advaitically-read declaration."],["Sivananda","uses this verse as an inspirational coda; the post-bondage state is described not as 'nothing' but as paramānanda."],["Krishnananda","vimalaḥ + paramānandam together signal sat-cit-ānanda; the body is gone, the bonds gone, but ānanda is positively present."],["Ayyangar","annotates kamalā-prabhu as identifying the realised one with Viṣṇu-as-paramātman."],["Deussen","marks v. 20 as the formal end of Section I's main exposition; vv. 21–29 are an Advaitic supplement, vv. 30–56 a separate composition."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 21","title":"Knowing the Self constantly — enjoy prārabdha without agitation","sa":["आत्मानं सततं ज्ञात्वा कालं नय महामते ।","प्रारब्धमखिलं भुञ्जन्नोद्वेगं कर्तुमर्हसि ॥ २१ ॥"],"iast":["ātmānaṃ satataṃ jñātvā kālaṃ naya mahāmate |","prārabdham akhilaṃ bhuñjann odvegaṃ kartum arhasi || 21 ||"],"words":[["ātmānam satatam jñātvā","m. acc. + adv. + ger.","constantly knowing the Self"],["kālam naya mahāmate","m. acc. + 2 sg. impv. + voc.","pass the time, O great-minded one"],["prārabdham akhilam bhuñjan","n. acc. + adj. + pres.part.","enjoying the entire prārabdha"],["na udvegam kartum arhasi","neg. + m. acc. + inf. + 2 sg. pres.","you ought not to be agitated"]],"lit":"“Constantly knowing the Self, pass the time, O great-minded one; enjoying the entire prārabdha, you ought not to be agitated.”","para":"To the realised disciple: 'Know the Self ceaselessly; let prārabdha play itself out; do not be disturbed.'  This opens the Advaita interlude (vv. 21–29).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","Section I's didactic frame returns here as direct address (mahāmate — vocative). Vv. 21–29 are a teacher's reassurance to the realised aspirant about ongoing prārabdha."],["Sivananda","an ethical instruction — equanimity towards prārabdha is the daily practice of the jīvanmukta. He cites this verse often."],["Krishnananda","udvegam kartum arhasi — 'you have no right to be agitated' — is technical: agitation would itself manifest a fresh karma, contradicting the realisation."],["Ayyangar","renders kālaṃ naya as 'pass thy days'."],["Deussen","the prārabdha excursus is here, in Section I, as a separate insertion; it parallels Yoga-Vāsiṣṭha III.114 and may share a source."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 22","title":"After tattva-realisation, no prārabdha is left","sa":["उत्पन्ने तत्त्वविज्ञाने प्रारब्धं नैव मुञ्चति ।","तत्त्वज्ञानोदयादूर्ध्वं प्रारब्धं नैव विद्यते ॥ २२ ॥"],"iast":["utpanne tattva-vijñāne prārabdhaṃ naiva muñcati |","tattva-jñānodayād ūrdhvaṃ prārabdhaṃ naiva vidyate || 22 ||"],"words":[["utpanne tattva-vijñāne","n. loc. abs.","when realisation of reality has arisen"],["prārabdham na eva muñcati","n. nom. + neg. + 3 sg. pres.","prārabdha does not (in fact) release (one)"],["tattva-jñāna-udayāt ūrdhvam","m. abl. + adv.","after the rising of tattva-jñāna"],["prārabdham na eva vidyate","n. nom. + neg. + 3 sg. pres.","prārabdha simply does not exist"]],"lit":"“When realisation of reality has arisen, prārabdha does not (truly) release (one); after the rising of tattva-jñāna, prārabdha simply does not exist.”","para":"The verse states the strong Advaita position: after tattva-jñāna, prārabdha is not a residue that drags on — there is no prārabdha at all. The reading is paradoxical against v. 21's apparent endorsement of prārabdha-enjoyment; vv. 23–29 reconcile them.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the first hemistich is the pūrva-pakṣa (the common view: prārabdha continues even for the jñānin); the second is the siddhānta (in fact prārabdha as a 'thing' is itself avidyā-construct)."],["Sivananda","the verse is paradoxical only at the literal level; it juxtaposes the empirical and absolute viewpoints to be reconciled in the following verses."],["Krishnananda","the radical strong-Advaita reading: not 'prārabdha persists for the jñānin' but 'prārabdha is itself avidyā-constructed and gone with avidyā'."],["Ayyangar","footnotes the standard tension with Pañcadaśī 7's prārabdha-doctrine."],["Deussen","the verse's strong claim is unusual; he marks it as the entry-point of the Advaitic interpolation."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 23","title":"As bodies vanish in waking, so on awakening","sa":["देहादीनामसत्त्वात्तु यथा स्वप्नो विबोधतः ।","कर्म जन्मान्तरीयं यत्प्रारब्धमिति कीर्तितम् ॥ २३ ॥"],"iast":["dehādīnām asattvāt tu yathā svapno vibodhataḥ |","karma janmāntarīyaṃ yat prārabdham iti kīrtitam || 23 ||"],"words":[["deha-ādīnām asattvāt tu","m. gen. pl. + n. abl. + particle","owing to the non-being of body etc."],["yathā svapnaḥ vibodhataḥ","conj. + m. nom. + m. abl.","as a dream (vanishes) from waking"],["karma janma-antarīyam yat","n. nom. + adj. + rel.","karma that belongs to another birth"],["prārabdham iti kīrtitam","n. nom. + quote + pp.","is what is called prārabdha"]],"lit":"“Because bodies etc. have no real being — as a dream (vanishes) on waking — what is called prārabdha is karma belonging to another birth.”","para":"Bodies are like dream-content: real only within the dream, gone with waking. 'Prārabdha' is a label for karma from a prior birth — and prior births depend on the very bodies that have no true being.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the dream-waking simile is the wedge that opens the strong reading: prārabdha rests on body-reality, which itself rests on avidyā; tattva-jñāna removes all three."],["Sivananda","useful for daily contemplation — 'this prārabdha is no more real than last night's dream'."],["Krishnananda","deha-asattva is the premise; everything that depends on it (including prārabdha) is dependent-existence."],["Ayyangar","renders janma-antarīyam as 'pertaining to a previous birth'."],["Deussen","compares Gauḍapāda Kārikā 2.6 — the same dream-analogy."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 24","title":"No 'other birth' for the Person — body too is superimposed","sa":["तत्तु जन्मान्तराभावात्पुंसो नैवास्ति कर्हिचित् ।","स्वप्नदेहो यथाध्यस्तस्तथैवायं हि देहकः ॥ २४ ॥"],"iast":["tat tu janmāntarābhāvāt puṃso naivāsti karhicit |","svapna-deho yathādhyastas tathaivāyaṃ hi dehakaḥ || 24 ||"],"words":[["tat tu janma-antara-abhāvāt","pron. + particle + n. abl.","but that, because of the absence of another birth"],["puṃsaḥ na eva asti karhicit","m. gen. + neg. + 3 sg. + adv.","is not for the Person at all, ever"],["svapna-dehaḥ yathā adhyastaḥ","m. nom. + conj. + pp.","as the dream-body (is) superimposed"],["tathā eva ayam hi dehakaḥ","adv. + pron. + emph. + m. nom.","just so (is) this body"]],"lit":"“But that (prārabdha), because there is no 'other birth' for the Person at all, never exists; as the dream-body is superimposed, so too is this body.”","para":"For the Self (puruṣa), there is no 'other birth'; thus no prārabdha. The dream-body is a clear case of superimposition (adhyāsa); the waking body is no different.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","puṃsaḥ — strong reading: 'for the (true) Person' — the Self — there has never been any birth at all. The line is near-Gauḍapādic."],["Sivananda","uses the verse for ātma-vicāra: 'where, in the changeless Self, is room for another birth?'"],["Krishnananda","adhyastaḥ = superimposed — the key Advaita term; v. 24 makes Section I's metaphysics explicit just before Section II's practical pivot."],["Ayyangar","footnotes the parallel to Śaṅkara's adhyāsa-bhāṣya."],["Deussen","marks v. 24 as the strongest ajāti-statement in the text."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 25","title":"No origin, no abiding — and the world's material cause","sa":["अध्यस्तस्य कुतो जन्म जन्माभावे कुतः स्थितिः ।","उपादानं प्रपञ्चस्य मृद्भाण्डस्येव पश्यति ॥ २५ ॥"],"iast":["adhyastasya kuto janma janmābhāve kutaḥ sthitiḥ |","upādānaṃ prapañcasya mṛd-bhāṇḍasyeva paśyati || 25 ||"],"words":[["adhyastasya kutaḥ janma","m. gen. + adv. + n. nom.","for the superimposed, whence (any) birth?"],["janma-abhāve kutaḥ sthitiḥ","n. loc. + adv. + f. nom.","in the absence of birth, whence (any) abiding?"],["upādānam prapañcasya","n. acc. + m. gen.","the material cause of the world"],["mṛd-bhāṇḍasya iva paśyati","n. gen. + indecl. + 3 sg. pres.","(one) sees as (of) a clay pot"]],"lit":"“For the superimposed, whence (any) birth? In the absence of birth, whence (any) continuance? One sees the material cause of the world as (one sees that of) a clay pot.”","para":"A superimposition has no birth; without birth, no continued existence. As clay is the material cause of a pot, so too the world's material cause must be seen — and what that 'clay' is, v. 26 will say.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the rhetorical questions are pūrva-pakṣa-clearing; the second hemistich introduces the upādāna-kāraṇa analysis that v. 26 develops."],["Sivananda","the mṛd-bhāṇḍa simile is Chāndogya 6.1.4 — the world's clay is to be revealed as Brahman + avidyā, just as the pot's clay is mere mṛt."],["Krishnananda","two stages of vicāra: question the birth of the superimposed; question its continuance; only then ask what its 'clay' is."],["Ayyangar","explicit footnote linking to Chāndogya 6.1.4."],["Deussen","the upādāna-question recasts the prārabdha-debate in Vivarta-vāda terms."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 26","title":"The Vedāntists say 'avidyā' — when it's destroyed, where is the world?","sa":["अज्ञानं चेति वेदान्तैस्तस्मिन्नष्टे क्व विश्वता ।","यथा रज्जुं परित्यज्य सर्पं गृह्णाति वै भ्रमात् ॥ २६ ॥"],"iast":["ajñānaṃ ceti vedāntais tasmin naṣṭe kva viśvatā |","yathā rajjuṃ parityajya sarpaṃ gṛhṇāti vai bhramāt || 26 ||"],"words":[["ajñānam ca iti vedāntaiḥ","n. nom. + quote + m. instr. pl.","'(it is) ignorance' — (so it is said) by the Vedāntists"],["tasmin naṣṭe kva viśvatā","loc. abs. + interr. + f. nom.","when that has perished, where (is) the world-character?"],["yathā rajjum parityajya","conj. + f. acc. + ger.","as, having abandoned the rope"],["sarpam gṛhṇāti vai bhramāt","m. acc. + 3 sg. + emph. + m. abl.","(one) grasps a snake, indeed, through error"]],"lit":"“'(That clay is) ignorance' — so (it is said) by the Vedāntists; when that has perished, where (is) the world-character? As, abandoning the rope, one grasps a snake out of error…”","para":"The 'clay' of the world is avidyā, as the Vedāntists teach. When avidyā is destroyed, where could 'world-ness' (viśvatā) remain? The famous rajju-sarpa (rope-snake) simile is introduced and will be unpacked in vv. 27–28.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","iti vedāntaiḥ = 'thus (the doctrine is held) by the Vedāntins' — Brahma-Yogin's marker that he is reporting the standard Advaita position, not opening a new one."],["Sivananda","the rope-snake simile begins here, but the verse breaks across the two hemistichs — a common Upaniṣadic device for sustained simile."],["Krishnananda","viśvatā (world-character, world-ness) is the abstract noun the verse uses; it is not viśva (the world-thing) that vanishes but the very being-a-world."],["Ayyangar","renders viśvatā as 'universality'."],["Deussen","the rajju-sarpa simile is here in its standard Advaita form; cf. Vedāntasāra §47."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 27","title":"The fool sees a snake; the wise see only rope","sa":["तद्वत्सत्यमविज्ञाय जगत्पश्यति मूढधीः ।","रज्जुखण्डे परिज्ञाते सर्परूपं न तिष्ठति ॥ २७ ॥"],"iast":["tadvat satyam avijñāya jagat paśyati mūḍhadhīḥ |","rajju-khaṇḍe parijñāte sarparūpaṃ na tiṣṭhati || 27 ||"],"words":[["tadvat satyam avijñāya","adv. + n. acc. + ger.","likewise, not knowing the truth"],["jagat paśyati mūḍha-dhīḥ","n. acc. + 3 sg. + m. nom.","the dull-minded one sees a 'world'"],["rajju-khaṇḍe parijñāte","m. loc. abs.","when the piece of rope is fully known"],["sarpa-rūpam na tiṣṭhati","n. nom. + neg. + 3 sg.","the snake-form does not abide"]],"lit":"“Likewise, not knowing the truth, the dull-minded one sees a 'world'; when the piece of rope is fully known, the snake-form does not abide.”","para":"The world appears to the unawakened the way a snake appears on a piece of rope. As soon as one knows the rope as rope, the apparent snake vanishes — and likewise the apparent world.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","parijñāte is in the loc. abs. — 'when (the rope) is fully known'. The Sanskrit is precise: it is parijñāna, not partial knowledge, that dispels the appearance."],["Sivananda","the practice corollary: don't just 'understand' the simile; actually see the rope-as-rope in one's own experience of the world."],["Krishnananda","the verse's structure shows the logical equivalence — the wisdom that dispels snake-cognition is the same kind as that which dispels world-cognition; nothing fancy is required."],["Ayyangar","renders mūḍha-dhīḥ as 'the deluded-minded'."],["Deussen","compares Vivekacūḍāmaṇi 110 for almost identical phrasing."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 28","title":"When substrate is known, world goes to emptiness — body too","sa":["अधिष्ठाने तथा ज्ञाते प्रपञ्चे शून्यतां गते ।","देहस्यापि प्रपञ्चत्वात्प्रारब्धावस्थितिः कृतः ॥ २८ ॥"],"iast":["adhiṣṭhāne tathā jñāte prapañce śūnyatāṃ gate |","dehasyāpi prapañcatvāt prārabdhāvasthitiḥ kṛtaḥ || 28 ||"],"words":[["adhiṣṭhāne tathā jñāte","n. loc. abs.","when the substrate (Brahman) is likewise known"],["prapañce śūnyatām gate","n. loc. abs.","when the prapañca has gone to emptiness"],["dehasya api prapañca-tvāt","m. gen. + emph. + n. abl.","since the body too is of the nature of prapañca"],["prārabdha-avasthitiḥ kṛtaḥ","f. nom. + m. nom. pp.","the abiding of prārabdha (is) (then) ‘made’ / posited"]],"lit":"“When the substrate (Brahman) is likewise known, when the prapañca has gone to emptiness — since the body too is of the nature of prapañca, the abiding of prārabdha (must) then (also be) posited (only by avidyā).”","para":"Once the Brahman-substrate is known and the world vanishes into 'emptiness' (śūnyatā), the body — being part of the world — vanishes too. Any 'continuance of prārabdha' is therefore a construct of avidyā, kept on for didactic reasons (see v. 29).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","śūnyatām here is not Mādhyamika śūnyatā but 'devoidness of independent reality'. The body's prapañca-character (= world-stuff-character) means it goes the way of the world; whence then prārabdha?"],["Sivananda","the verse establishes the radical-Advaita reading; v. 29 will provide the upādhi-level reading that reconciles v. 21."],["Krishnananda","śūnyatā here is to be read with Vedānta caution — it is the 'voiding' of independent reality, not an absolute void."],["Ayyangar","footnotes the apparent overlap with Mādhyamika śūnyatā but distinguishes the readings."],["Deussen","the use of śūnyatām is unusual and may indicate later Mahāyāna-influenced reformulation."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 29","title":"'Prārabdha' is taught only for the ignorant — when time exhausts it…","sa":["अज्ञानजनबोधार्थं प्रारब्धमिति चोच्यते ।","ततः कालवशादेव प्रारब्धे तु क्षयं गते ॥ २९ ॥"],"iast":["ajñāna-jana-bodhārthaṃ prārabdham iti cocyate |","tataḥ kālavaśād eva prārabdhe tu kṣayaṃ gate || 29 ||"],"words":[["ajñāna-jana-bodha-artham","n. acc. sg. dat.purp.","for the instruction of ignorant people"],["prārabdham iti ca ucyate","n. + quote + 3 sg. pass.","is 'prārabdha' (so) said"],["tataḥ kāla-vaśāt eva","adv. + m. abl.","then, by the power of time itself"],["prārabdhe tu kṣayam gate","n. loc. abs.","when prārabdha has gone to extinction"]],"lit":"“For the instruction of ignorant people, it is said: 'prārabdha (exists)'. Then, by the power of time itself, when prārabdha has gone to extinction…” (verse continues into v. 30)","para":"The doctrine 'prārabdha persists' is taught only as a teaching device for the unawakened. When time, by its own course, exhausts prārabdha — the next verse (30) describes what happens. This is the structural hinge from Section I to Section II.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","ajñāna-jana-bodha-artham — for the instruction of the ignorant — is the standard Advaita defence of the prārabdha-doctrine: it is upacāra, said for didactic purposes, not paramārtha. The hemistich starting tataḥ kāla-vaśāt eva is the actual transition into Section II."],["Sivananda","Section I concludes by undercutting its own central doctrine (prārabdha), then immediately pivots to practice: time exhausts what doctrine could not."],["Krishnananda","the verse is the bridge — the Advaita interlude (vv. 21–28) ends with this 'pedagogical concession', and the practical yoga of Section II begins."],["Ayyangar","annotates the explicit pedagogy-confession: prārabdha is a teaching aid."],["Deussen","marks v. 29 as the end of his proposed interpolated Advaita stratum; from v. 30 the older yoga-stratum resumes."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 30","title":"From praṇava arises the luminous Nāda — Self emerges like the sun","sa":["ब्रह्मप्रणवसन्धानं नादो ज्योतिर्मयः शिवः ।","स्वयमाविर्भवेदात्मा मेघापायेऽंशुमानिव ॥ ३० ॥"],"iast":["brahma-praṇava-sandhānaṃ nādo jyotirmayaḥ śivaḥ |","svayam āvirbhaved ātmā meghāpāye 'ṃśumān iva || 30 ||"],"words":[["brahma-praṇava-sandhānam","n. nom. sg.","(by) the joining-with the Brahma-praṇava (Oṃ)"],["nādaḥ jyotir-mayaḥ śivaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(arises) the nāda, made-of-light, auspicious"],["svayam āvirbhavet ātmā","adv. + 3 sg. pot. + m. nom.","the Self would (then) become manifest of itself"],["megha-apāye aṃśumān iva","m. loc. + m. nom. + indecl.","as the sun on the dispersal of clouds"]],"lit":"“By the joining-with the Brahma-praṇava (Oṃ) (arises) the nāda — made of light, auspicious; (and) the Self becomes manifest of its own accord, as the sun (does) on the dispersal of clouds.”","para":"When the meditator unites with the Praṇava, an inner luminous Sound (nāda) arises, and the Self emerges of itself — like the sun emerging when clouds disperse. This verse is the formal opening of Section II.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","brahma-praṇava-sandhāna is technical: 'making-the-junction-with-the-Brahman-praṇava'. The yogi does not 'produce' the nāda; nāda manifests as a side-effect of correct praṇava-yoga."],["Sivananda","the cloud-sun simile is the signature image of jīvanmukti — the Self was never absent, only obscured."],["Krishnananda","svayam — 'of itself' — is the verse's force. The Self's manifestation is uncaused and unsought; the practice merely removes the cover."],["Ayyangar","renders aṃśumān as 'the radiant one'."],["Deussen","compares Bṛhad. 4.3.6 for the cloud-sun simile."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 31","title":"Siddhāsana, Vaiṣṇavī-mudrā, right ear — the standard nāda posture","sa":["सिद्धासने स्थितो योगी मुद्रां सन्धाय वैष्णवीम् ।","शृणुयाद्दक्षिणे कर्णे नादमन्तर्गतं सदा ॥ ३१ ॥"],"iast":["siddhāsane sthito yogī mudrāṃ sandhāya vaiṣṇavīm |","śṛṇuyād dakṣiṇe karṇe nādam antargataṃ sadā || 31 ||"],"words":[["siddhāsane sthitaḥ yogī","n. loc. + m. nom.","the yogi, seated in siddhāsana"],["mudrām sandhāya vaiṣṇavīm","f. acc. + ger. + f. acc.","having formed the Vaiṣṇavī-mudrā"],["śṛṇuyāt dakṣiṇe karṇe","3 sg. pot. + n. loc. sg.","should listen, in the right ear"],["nādam antar-gatam sadā","m. acc. + pp. + adv.","to the nāda gone-within, always"]],"lit":"“The yogi, seated in siddhāsana and having formed the Vaiṣṇavī-mudrā, should always listen — in the right ear — to the nāda that has gone within.”","para":"Technical setting: siddhāsana posture; Vaiṣṇavī-mudrā (steady gaze without object); attention placed at the right ear; listening for the inner nāda. Repeated by HYP 4.67–68.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","Vaiṣṇavī-mudrā here = śāmbhavī-mudrā (gaze externally directed, mind internally absorbed). Why the right ear specifically: the right side is solar (piṅgalā) and is the conventional dakṣiṇa-channel for nāda-reception in this tradition."],["Sivananda","exact instruction repeated in his Yoga Asanas and the nāda-chapter of Kundalini Yoga; close ears with fingers if needed at first; then listen without closing."],["Krishnananda","antargatam — 'gone within' — emphasises that the nāda is not 'produced' but 'turned-into-the-interior'. The yoga turns the ear inward, not outward."],["Ayyangar","footnotes the Haṭha-Yoga-Pradīpikā 4.67 parallel and the relation to ṣaṇmukhī-mudrā."],["Deussen","his nāda-yoga section opens here; compares Yogatattva 102."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 32","title":"Practised nāda covers the outer sound; conquering the senses, one reaches Turīya","sa":["अभ्यस्यमानो नादोऽयं बाह्यमावृणुते ध्वनिम् ।","पक्षाद्विपक्षमखिलं जित्वा तुर्यपदं व्रजेत् ॥ ३२ ॥"],"iast":["abhyasyamāno nādo 'yaṃ bāhyam āvṛṇute dhvanim |","pakṣād vipakṣam akhilaṃ jitvā turyapadaṃ vrajet || 32 ||"],"words":[["abhyasyamānaḥ nādaḥ ayam","m. nom. pres.pass.part. + m. nom. + pron.","this nāda, being cultivated"],["bāhyam āvṛṇute dhvanim","m. acc. + 3 sg. pres. mid. + m. acc.","covers the external sound"],["pakṣāt vipakṣam akhilam","m. abl. + m. acc.","from (any) side, all opposition"],["jitvā turya-padam vrajet","ger. + n. acc. + 3 sg. pot.","having conquered, would go to the Turīya state"]],"lit":"“This nāda, being cultivated, covers (drowns out) the external sound; having conquered all opposition from any side (pakṣa-vipakṣa), one would go to the state of Turīya.”","para":"As practice deepens, the inner nāda overwhelms external sound. Having conquered every opposing pull (pakṣa-vipakṣa = thought-counterthought, sense-resistance), the yogi reaches Turīya.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","āvṛṇute — 'envelops' — describes the masking effect: the inner sound, once cultivated, mutes the outer. Pakṣa-vipakṣa is reasoned alternation (cf. Amṛta-Bindu v. 6's pakṣapāta)."],["Sivananda","an empirical observation: the practitioner notices the outer environment grow quieter as nāda strengthens. This is not auditory but attentional."],["Krishnananda","Turīya here is named for the first time in the text — Section II's goal is identified explicitly."],["Ayyangar","renders pakṣa-vipakṣa as 'opposing dvandvas'."],["Deussen","compares HYP 4.69 for the same statement of the masking effect."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 33","title":"In early practice, many gross nādas — they refine over time","sa":["श्रूयते प्रथमाभ्यासे नादो नानाविधो महान् ।","वर्धमानस्तथाभ्यासे श्रूयते सूक्ष्मसूक्ष्मतः ॥ ३३ ॥"],"iast":["śrūyate prathamābhyāse nādo nānāvidho mahān |","vardhamānas tathābhyāse śrūyate sūkṣma-sūkṣmataḥ || 33 ||"],"words":[["śrūyate prathama-abhyāse","3 sg. pres. pass. + m. loc.","is heard, in the first practice"],["nādaḥ nānā-vidhaḥ mahān","m. nom. + adj. + adj.","(a) loud, various-kinds nāda"],["vardhamānaḥ tathā abhyāse","m. nom. + adv. + m. loc.","(but) growing-better with practice"],["śrūyate sūkṣma-sūkṣmataḥ","3 sg. pass. + adv.","is heard ever-more-subtly"]],"lit":"“In the first practice, a great nāda of many kinds is heard; (then) growing (in skill) with practice, it is heard ever-more-subtly.”","para":"Initially many loud and varied inner sounds are heard. With sustained practice they refine and grow progressively subtler.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","v. 33 sets up the famous list in vv. 34–35; the principle is that early-practice nādas are gross, advanced-practice nādas are subtle."],["Sivananda","early practitioners often complain that the inner sound is overpowering; v. 33 reassures — that is the entry-level experience."],["Krishnananda","vardhamānaḥ refers to the yogi's growth, not the sound's volume — the sound becomes subtler as the listener becomes finer."],["Ayyangar","renders sūkṣma-sūkṣmataḥ as 'progressively subtler' (the adv. is an unusual compound)."],["Deussen","compares HYP 4.84 for the gross-to-subtle progression."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 34","title":"First — ocean, cloud, kettle-drum, waterfall; then mardala and bell-trumpet","sa":["आदौ जलधिजीमूतभेरीनिर्झरसम्भवः ।","मध्ये मर्दलशब्दाभो घण्टाकाहलजस्तथा ॥ ३४ ॥"],"iast":["ādau jaladhi-jīmūta-bherī-nirjhara-sambhavaḥ |","madhye mardala-śabdābho ghaṇṭā-kāhala-jas tathā || 34 ||"],"words":[["ādau","m. loc. sg.","in the beginning"],["jaladhi-jīmūta-bherī-nirjhara-sambhavaḥ","m. nom. sg. bahuvr.","arising-from ocean, cloud, kettle-drum, waterfall"],["madhye","n. loc. sg.","in the middle"],["mardala-śabda-ābhaḥ","m. nom. sg. kc.","resembling the sound of the mardala-drum"],["ghaṇṭā-kāhala-jaḥ tathā","m. nom. + adv.","and arising-from-bell-and-trumpet"]],"lit":"“In the beginning, (the nāda) arises-as ocean, cloud, kettle-drum, waterfall; in the middle, like the sound of the mardala-drum and arising-as bell and trumpet.”","para":"Early stage of nāda-practice: the inner sound is gross — like the ocean's roar, the rumbling of clouds, a kettle-drum, a waterfall. Middle stage: like a tabla-drum (mardala), a temple-bell, or a long-trumpet (kāhala).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the 'beginning' and 'middle' here are not chronological stages but levels of inner perception; a practitioner may pass through all of them within one sitting."],["Sivananda","the seven-or-ten-nāda list begins here; he memorises it and teaches it as a diagnostic for what stage the student has reached."],["Krishnananda","the gross sounds are 'environmental' in character (ocean, waterfall, drum); the subtle sounds (v. 35) are 'instrumental' (vīṇā, flute) — the progression is from open-air to fine-instrument."],["Ayyangar","footnotes the precise tradition-instruments (mardala = pakhāvaj-like drum; kāhala = long ceremonial trumpet)."],["Deussen","lists the same set with mild ordering variation in HYP 4.84."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 35","title":"At the end — bell, bamboo-flute, vīṇā, and bee — heard subtly","sa":["अन्ते तु किङ्किणीवंशवीणाभ्रमरनिःस्वनः ।","इति नानाविधा नादाः श्रूयन्ते सूक्ष्मसूक्ष्मतः ॥ ३५ ॥"],"iast":["ante tu kiṅkiṇī-vaṃśa-vīṇā-bhramara-niḥsvanaḥ |","iti nānāvidhā nādāḥ śrūyante sūkṣma-sūkṣmataḥ || 35 ||"],"words":[["ante tu","m. loc. + particle","but at the end"],["kiṅkiṇī-vaṃśa-vīṇā-bhramara-niḥsvanaḥ","m. nom. sg. kc.","(arises) the sound of small-bell, bamboo-flute, vīṇā, bee"],["iti nānāvidhāḥ nādāḥ","indecl. + m. nom. pl.","thus various-kinds nādas"],["śrūyante sūkṣma-sūkṣmataḥ","3 pl. pres. pass. + adv.","are heard progressively-subtly"]],"lit":"“But at the end, (arises) the sound of small-bell, bamboo-flute, vīṇā, and bee — thus various nādas are heard, progressively more subtly.”","para":"Final stage of the gross-to-subtle progression: the inner sound is now like a small temple-bell (kiṅkiṇī), a bamboo flute (vaṃśa), a vīṇā, and finally a bee (bhramara). The bhramara-stage is the closest to the soundless final dissolution.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the ten-sound list (jaladhi → bhramara) is canonical for this Upaniṣad and quoted verbatim by Haṭha-Yoga-Pradīpikā. Bhramara is the last, just before the sound vanishes altogether."],["Sivananda","the bhramara-stage marks the transition from listening to laya; once you hear the bee, you are at the edge of dissolution."],["Krishnananda","the progression internalises: ocean → waterfall (atmospheric); drum → trumpet (artefacts at distance); bell → flute (close artefacts); vīṇā → bee (almost-bodily)."],["Ayyangar","renders niḥsvanaḥ as 'humming' for bhramara."],["Deussen","compares HYP 4.85 for the identical list with the same final bee."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 36","title":"When you hear the loud, lay hold of the subtle within it","sa":["महति श्रूयमाणे तु महाभेर्यादिकध्वनौ ।","तत्र सूक्ष्मं सूक्ष्मतरं नादमेव परामृशेत् ॥ ३६ ॥"],"iast":["mahati śrūyamāṇe tu mahābheryādika-dhvanau |","tatra sūkṣmaṃ sūkṣmataraṃ nādam eva parāmṛśet || 36 ||"],"words":[["mahati śrūyamāṇe tu","m. loc. abs.","but when the great (sound) is being heard"],["mahā-bherī-ādika-dhvanau","m. loc. sg.","in the sound of great-drum etc."],["tatra sūkṣmam sūkṣmataram","adv. + n. acc.","there, (one should attend to) the subtler-than-subtle"],["nādam eva parāmṛśet","m. acc. + 3 sg. pot.","the (very) nāda one should grasp / attend to"]],"lit":"“But when the great (sound) — that of the mahā-bherī etc. — is being heard, there one should grasp the subtler-than-subtle nāda itself.”","para":"Practical instruction: when you are aware of a loud inner sound (mahā-bherī etc.), don't dwell on its loudness — listen through it for the subtler nāda embedded within.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","parāmṛśet — 'grasp through reflection' — the verb is technical for the introspective seizing of the inner referent."],["Sivananda","the practical hinge of the entire practice: don't be content with the loud nāda; always probe the next, subtler layer."],["Krishnananda","the verse warns against satisfaction with auditory experience — the goal is dissolution, not collection of sound-phenomena."],["Ayyangar","compares HYP 4.86 — the same instruction in nearly identical wording."],["Deussen","marks v. 36 as the methodological hub of Section II."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 37","title":"Move freely between gross and subtle; let the mind not stray elsewhere","sa":["घनमुत्सृज्य वा सूक्ष्मे सूक्ष्ममुत्सृज्य वा घने ।","रममाणमपि क्षिप्तं मनो नान्यत्र चालयेत् ॥ ३७ ॥"],"iast":["ghanam utsṛjya vā sūkṣme sūkṣmam utsṛjya vā ghane |","ramamāṇam api kṣiptaṃ mano nānyatra cālayet || 37 ||"],"words":[["ghanam utsṛjya vā sūkṣme","m. acc. + ger. + conj. + n. loc.","either having given up the gross, in the subtle"],["sūkṣmam utsṛjya vā ghane","n. acc. + ger. + conj. + m. loc.","or having given up the subtle, in the gross"],["ramamāṇam api kṣiptam","n. acc. pres.part. + emph. + pp.","(though) playing-or-distracted"],["manaḥ na anyatra cālayet","n. acc. + neg. + 3 sg. pot.","the mind one should not move elsewhere"]],"lit":"“Either, having given up the gross, (resting) in the subtle, or having given up the subtle, (resting) in the gross — though the mind is playing or distracted, one should not move it elsewhere (i.e. away from nāda).”","para":"In practice you may shuttle between gross and subtle nādas. Even if the mind plays freely or gets distracted, do not let it leave the nāda-domain altogether — keep it within sound, whatever the level.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the rule is permissive at the level of grossness but strict at the level of domain — within nāda, free; outside nāda, never."],["Sivananda","his practical commentary: 'oscillate within nāda, never leave nāda'."],["Krishnananda","the verse acknowledges the natural restlessness of the mind and works with it rather than against it; the only constraint is the domain."],["Ayyangar","renders kṣiptam as 'tossed-about'."],["Deussen","compares HYP 4.88 for the same gross/subtle oscillation rule."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 38","title":"Wherever mind first catches nāda, become steady there and dissolve","sa":["यत्र कुत्रापि वा नादे लगति प्रथमं मनः ।","तत्र तत्र स्थिरीभूत्वा तेन सार्धं विलीयते ॥ ३८ ॥"],"iast":["yatra kutrāpi vā nāde lagati prathamaṃ manaḥ |","tatra tatra sthirībhūtvā tena sārdhaṃ vilīyate || 38 ||"],"words":[["yatra kutrāpi vā nāde","rel. + indecl. + conj. + m. loc.","in whatever nāda whatsoever"],["lagati prathamam manaḥ","3 sg. pres. + adv. + n. nom.","the mind first catches"],["tatra tatra sthirī-bhūtvā","adv. + ger. of √bhū","becoming steady right there"],["tena sārdham vilīyate","m. instr. + indecl. + 3 sg. pres. pass.","dissolves together with that"]],"lit":"“In whatever nāda (of any sort) the mind first catches hold, becoming steady right there, (the mind) dissolves together with that.”","para":"Whichever nāda the mind first attaches to, make it your seat. Become utterly steady there, and the mind will dissolve right into that nāda.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","tena sārdham vilīyate — 'dissolves together with that' — is laya proper. The 'with that' is essential: the mind doesn't dissolve alone, but with its object-nāda; both go together."],["Sivananda","the verse legitimises individual variation — no one nāda is universally correct; the first hook is the one to ride."],["Krishnananda","the practical insight: every nāda is a portal, and the mind needs only one. Choosing among nādas is not necessary."],["Ayyangar","renders lagati as 'fastens on'."],["Deussen","compares Yoga-Vāsiṣṭha III.121 on the same 'first-catch' principle."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 39","title":"Forgetting the outer, mind merges with nāda — and dissolves in cidākāśa","sa":["विस्मृत्य सकलं बाह्यं नादे दुग्धाम्बुवन्मनः ।","एकीभूयाथ सहसा चिदाकाशे विलीयते ॥ ३९ ॥"],"iast":["vismṛtya sakalaṃ bāhyaṃ nāde dugdhāmbuvan manaḥ |","ekī-bhūyātha sahasā cidākāśe vilīyate || 39 ||"],"words":[["vismṛtya sakalam bāhyam","ger. + n. acc.","forgetting the entire external"],["nāde dugdha-ambu-vat","m. loc. + adv.","in the nāda, as milk-and-water"],["manaḥ ekī-bhūya atha sahasā","n. nom. + ger. + adv.","the mind, having become one, then suddenly"],["cidākāśe vilīyate","m. loc. + 3 sg. pres. pass.","dissolves in the cit-space"]],"lit":"“Forgetting the entire external (world), the mind becomes one with the nāda as milk (mixes) with water; then, suddenly, it dissolves in the cit-space.”","para":"The outer world forgotten, the mind merges with nāda as milk merges with water — and then, all at once, dissolves into cidākāśa (the space of pure consciousness).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the milk-water simile is precise: not 'mixed' (which keeps two substances) but ekī-bhūya — 'become one'. The cidākāśe vilīyate is the final laya."],["Sivananda","perhaps the most quoted verse of the text in modern nāda-yoga literature."],["Krishnananda","atha sahasā — 'then, suddenly' — the final dissolution is not gradual; the preparation is gradual, the result is instantaneous."],["Ayyangar","translates cidākāśa as 'the ether of consciousness'."],["Deussen","compares Yoga-Cūḍāmaṇi 36 for the milk-water simile."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 40","title":"Become indifferent — practise contemplating the unmanī-making Nāda","sa":["उदासीनस्ततो भूत्वा सदाभ्यासेन संयमी ।","उन्मनीकारकं सद्यो नादमेवावधारयेत् ॥ ४० ॥"],"iast":["udāsīnas tato bhūtvā sadābhyāsena saṃyamī |","unmanī-kārakaṃ sadyo nādam evāvadhārayet || 40 ||"],"words":[["udāsīnaḥ tataḥ bhūtvā","m. nom. + adv. + ger.","then having become indifferent"],["sadā-abhyāsena saṃyamī","m. instr. + m. nom.","(being) restrained by constant practice"],["unmanī-kārakam sadyaḥ","n. acc. + adv.","(the) at-once unmanī-making (state-of-no-mind-producing)"],["nādam eva avadhārayet","m. acc. + 3 sg. pot.","the very nāda (one) should hold-in-mind"]],"lit":"“Then, having become indifferent (to everything else), the restrained (yogi), by constant practice, should hold in mind the very nāda that immediately produces the unmanī state.”","para":"The yogi, indifferent to all else and steady through constant practice, fixes attention on the particular nāda that instantly induces unmanī (the no-mind state).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","unmanī-kāraka — 'producing the unmanī' — is the specific quality of the truly subtle nāda. The 'sadyaḥ' (at once) marks the final stage."],["Sivananda","unmanī is the transcendent state where the mind ceases to function as mind; it is the goal of Section II."],["Krishnananda","udāsīna is a key Vedānta term — 'sitting-apart', not active but not asleep; the mood of the laya-stage."],["Ayyangar","compares the unmanī-state of Yoga-Cūḍāmaṇi and HYP 4.20."],["Deussen","the unmanī-doctrine here is technical, drawn from the same source as Maṇḍala-Brāhmaṇa."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 41","title":"Cast off thoughts and effort; meditate on nāda — mind dissolves there","sa":["सर्वचिन्तां समुत्सृज्य सर्वचेष्टाविवर्जितः ।","नादमेवानुसन्दध्यान्नादे चित्तं विलीयते ॥ ४१ ॥"],"iast":["sarva-cintāṃ samutsṛjya sarva-ceṣṭā-vivarjitaḥ |","nādam evānusandadhyān nāde cittaṃ vilīyate || 41 ||"],"words":[["sarva-cintām samutsṛjya","f. acc. + ger.","completely giving up all thought"],["sarva-ceṣṭā-vivarjitaḥ","m. nom. sg. bahuvr.","free of all effort"],["nādam eva anusandadhyāt","m. acc. + 3 sg. pot. of anu-sam-dhā","(one) should meditatively unite with the very nāda"],["nāde cittam vilīyate","m. loc. + n. nom. + 3 sg. pres. pass.","the citta dissolves in the nāda"]],"lit":"“Casting away all thought, free of all effort, (one) should meditatively unite with the very nāda; (and) the citta dissolves in the nāda.”","para":"Drop every thought; cease all striving; perform nāda-anusandhāna alone; the citta dissolves into the nāda.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","anusandadhyāt is the technical verb-form of anu-sandhāna — the sustained focal-uniting. It is not ordinary dhyāna but the specific 'after-following-and-joining' of the laya tradition."],["Sivananda","v. 41 is the operational summary of the whole practice: drop thought, drop effort, do nāda-anusandhāna, citta dissolves."],["Krishnananda","ceṣṭā-vivarjitaḥ — free of all activity including the activity of focusing — is what distinguishes laya from dhāraṇā."],["Ayyangar","renders anusandadhyāt as 'should contemplate steadily'."],["Deussen","compares HYP 4.81."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 42","title":"Bee drinking honey ignores all scents — citta on nāda ignores all objects","sa":["मकरन्दं पिबन्भृङ्गो गन्धान्नापेक्षते तथा ।","नादासक्तं सदा चित्तं विषयं न हि काङ्क्षति ॥ ४२ ॥"],"iast":["makarandaṃ piban bhṛṅgo gandhān nāpekṣate tathā |","nādāsaktaṃ sadā cittaṃ viṣayaṃ na hi kāṅkṣati || 42 ||"],"words":[["makarandam piban bhṛṅgaḥ","m. acc. + pres.part. + m. nom.","the bee, drinking the honey"],["gandhān na apekṣate tathā","m. acc. pl. + neg. + 3 sg. pres. + adv.","does not regard the scents; likewise"],["nāda-āsaktam sadā cittam","n. nom. + adv. + n. nom.","the citta always-attached-to-nāda"],["viṣayam na hi kāṅkṣati","m. acc. + neg. + emph. + 3 sg. pres.","does not at all desire (any) object"]],"lit":"“The bee, drinking the honey, does not regard the scents; likewise the citta, always attached to nāda, does not at all desire (any) object.”","para":"The bee deep in honey ignores even the flowers' scents around it. So too the mind absorbed in nāda has no longing for any sense-object.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the bhṛṅga-makaranda simile is signature for this Upaniṣad. The bee is the citta, the honey is the nāda; once absorbed in the honey, the bee ignores even the floral scents — i.e. the citta, absorbed in nāda, ignores even the most attractive sense-objects."],["Sivananda","the practical simile of the entire text — when the inner sweetness is found, outer sweetnesses lose their pull. Used at every laya-yoga retreat."],["Krishnananda","the verse establishes the natural vairāgya that accompanies nāda-absorption — not effortful renunciation but spontaneous indifference."],["Ayyangar","renders bhṛṅga as 'black-bee'."],["Deussen","compares Yoga-Cūḍāmaṇi 41 for the bhṛṅga-makaranda."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 43","title":"Bound by the perfume of nāda, the inner serpent (mind) is at once tamed","sa":["बद्धः सुनादगन्धेन सद्यः सन्त्यक्तचापलः ।","नादग्रहणतश्चित्तमन्तरङ्गभुजङ्गमः ॥ ४३ ॥"],"iast":["baddhaḥ sunāda-gandhena sadyaḥ santyakta-cāpalaḥ |","nāda-grahaṇataś cittam antaraṅga-bhujaṅgamaḥ || 43 ||"],"words":[["baddhaḥ su-nāda-gandhena","m. nom. + m. instr. kc.","bound by the perfume of the fine nāda"],["sadyaḥ santyakta-cāpalaḥ","adv. + m. nom. bahuvr.","at once, having abandoned fickleness"],["nāda-grahaṇataḥ cittam","abl. + n. nom.","from the grasping-of-nāda, the citta"],["antaraṅga-bhujaṅgamaḥ","m. nom. sg. kc.","(is) the inner serpent (tamed)"]],"lit":"“Bound by the perfume of the fine nāda, (the citta-serpent) at once abandons (its) fickleness; through the grasping-of-nāda the citta — the inner serpent — (is tamed).”","para":"The mind is an inner serpent — restless, darting. Bound by the fragrance of the sweet inner sound (su-nāda-gandha), it abandons its volatility at once. This continues into v. 44 with the elephant simile.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","antaraṅga-bhujaṅgama — 'inner-serpent' — is a striking image: the citta is like a snake (cf. cetana-bhujaṅgī of Tantra), normally venomous, here charmed by the inner sound."],["Sivananda","the snake-charm simile is paired with v. 44's elephant-goad — two images for the same effect: the wild mind subdued by the inner nāda."],["Krishnananda","santyakta-cāpalaḥ — 'having abandoned fickleness' — fickleness (cāpala) is the citta's signature; nāda is its specific antidote."],["Ayyangar","renders cāpala as 'restlessness'."],["Deussen","the bhujaṅgama-image is rare in earlier Upaniṣads; he notes the late-Yoga flavour."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 44","title":"The mind, forgetting all, becomes one-pointed and runs nowhere","sa":["विस्मृत्य विश्वमेकाग्रः कुत्रचिन्न हि धावति ।","मनोमत्तगजेन्द्रस्य विषयोद्यानचारिणः ॥ ४४ ॥"],"iast":["vismṛtya viśvam ekāgraḥ kutracin na hi dhāvati |","mano-matta-gajendrasya viṣayodyāna-cāriṇaḥ || 44 ||"],"words":[["vismṛtya viśvam ekāgraḥ","ger. + n. acc. + m. nom.","forgetting the universe, one-pointed"],["kutracit na hi dhāvati","adv. + neg. + emph. + 3 sg. pres.","does not run anywhere at all"],["mano-matta-gaja-indrasya","m. gen. sg. kc.","of the mad-elephant-lord of the mind"],["viṣaya-udyāna-cāriṇaḥ","m. gen. sg. bahuvr.","(which is) wandering in the garden of sense-objects"]],"lit":"“Forgetting the universe, one-pointed, (the mind) does not run anywhere at all — of the mad-elephant-lord of the mind, which wanders in the garden of sense-objects…” (continues into v. 45)","para":"Mind, having forgotten the universe and become one-pointed, runs nowhere. The mind is like a mad bull-elephant rampaging in a pleasure-garden — and the nāda is the goad that tames it (v. 45).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the mad-elephant simile complements the snake simile of v. 43; the verse pair captures two different modes of the wild mind — coiled fickleness and rampaging force."],["Sivananda","the verse stages a vivid image; he expands it into a sermon on the mind's normal rampaging in the garden of senses."],["Krishnananda","vismṛtya viśvam ekāgraḥ — the order is significant: first forget the universe, then become one-pointed; not the other way around."],["Ayyangar","renders matta-gajendra as 'rutting elephant'."],["Deussen","compares Vivekacūḍāmaṇi 199 — the same elephant-of-the-mind image."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 45","title":"Nāda — the sharp goad and the snare for the elephant and the deer","sa":["नियामनसमर्थोऽयं निनादो निशिताङ्कुशः ।","नादोऽन्तरङ्गसारङ्गबन्धने वागुरायते ॥ ४५ ॥"],"iast":["niyāmana-samartho 'yaṃ ninādo niśitāṅkuśaḥ |","nādo 'ntaraṅga-sāraṅga-bandhane vāgurāyate || 45 ||"],"words":[["niyāmana-samarthaḥ ayam","m. nom. + pron.","this (nāda) (is) capable-of-restraint"],["ninādaḥ niśita-aṅkuśaḥ","m. nom.","the resonating-nāda (is) a sharp goad"],["nādaḥ antaraṅga-sāraṅga-bandhane","m. nom. + n. loc. kc.","the nāda, in the binding of the inner-deer"],["vāgurāyate","3 sg. denom. pres. of vāgurā","acts as a snare-net"]],"lit":"“This resonating nāda is capable of restraining (the mad mind-elephant) — (it is) a sharp goad; the nāda, in binding the inner deer (= the mind), acts as a snare-net.”","para":"The inner Nāda is the goad for the elephant-mind and the net for the deer-mind — the practitioner's only effective instrument of restraint.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","vāgurāyate (acts as a snare) is denominative — the nāda 'snare-nets' the inner deer. The shift from gross instrument (goad) to net (snare) parallels the move from gross to subtle nādas."],["Sivananda","the verse is his pedagogical centrepiece: every yoga teacher has tried to restrain the mind by other means, but only nāda is sufficient."],["Krishnananda","the variety of restraint-images (snake-charm, elephant-goad, deer-snare) is not accidental — different aspirants will resonate with different ones."],["Ayyangar","renders sāraṅga as 'spotted deer'."],["Deussen","the sāraṅga is a śloka-only image; the chapter's cumulative effect is a bestiary of inner restraint."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 46","title":"In the inner ocean's restraint, nāda is the shore","sa":["अन्तरङ्गसमुद्रस्य रोधे वेलायतेऽपि च ।","ब्रह्मप्रणवसंलग्ननादो ज्योतिर्मयात्मकः ॥ ४६ ॥"],"iast":["antaraṅga-samudrasya rodhe velāyate 'pi ca |","brahma-praṇava-saṃlagna-nādo jyotirmayātmakaḥ || 46 ||"],"words":[["antaraṅga-samudrasya rodhe","m. gen. + m. loc.","in the obstructing of the inner ocean"],["velāyate api ca","3 sg. denom. pres. + emph.","(the nāda) acts as the shore"],["brahma-praṇava-saṃlagna-nādaḥ","m. nom. sg. kc.","the nāda joined-with-the-Brahma-praṇava"],["jyotir-maya-ātmakaḥ","m. nom. sg. kc.","is light-natured-in-essence"]],"lit":"“In the obstructing of the inner ocean (of citta-vṛttis), (the nāda) acts as the shore; (this) nāda joined-with-the-Brahma-praṇava is luminous in its very essence.”","para":"The mind is an inner ocean of waves; the nāda — joined to Praṇava and luminous by nature — is the shore that bounds it. The bestiary of vv. 43–45 gives way to a sea-and-shore image.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","velāyate (acts as shore) is denominative — the nāda is not merely a thing-on-the-shore but the shore-function itself; it bounds the ocean by being-the-bound."],["Sivananda","complements the bhramara of v. 42 — the citta is both the bee (seeking) and the ocean (overflowing); the nāda is both the honey and the shore."],["Krishnananda","the chain of similes is now complete: snake-elephant-deer-ocean — the citta in every wild aspect, the nāda its specific restraint each time."],["Ayyangar","renders velā as 'sea-shore'."],["Deussen","marks v. 46 as a transitional summary; vv. 47–49 will move from imagery to direct technical exposition."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 47","title":"Mind dissolves there — Viṣṇu's supreme abode; as long as sound, so long ākāśa-saṅkalpa","sa":["मनस्तत्र लयं याति तद्विष्णोः परमं पदम् ।","तावदाकाशसङ्कल्पो यावच्छब्दः प्रवर्तते ॥ ४७ ॥"],"iast":["manas tatra layaṃ yāti tad-viṣṇoḥ paramaṃ padam |","tāvad ākāśa-saṅkalpo yāvac chabdaḥ pravartate || 47 ||"],"words":[["manaḥ tatra layam yāti","n. nom. + adv. + m. acc. + 3 sg.","the mind goes there to dissolution"],["tat-viṣṇoḥ paramam padam","n. + m. gen. + adj. + n.","that (is) Viṣṇu's supreme abode"],["tāvat ākāśa-saṅkalpaḥ","correl. + m. nom.","so long the 'ākāśa-conception'"],["yāvat śabdaḥ pravartate","correl. + m. nom. + 3 sg.","as long as sound is operating"]],"lit":"“The mind goes there to dissolution — that is Viṣṇu's supreme abode. So long is the 'ākāśa-conception' (the conception of space) as sound is operating.”","para":"In nāda the mind dissolves — there, Viṣṇu's paramaṃ padam. The very conception of 'space' lasts only as long as sound is in operation; when sound ceases, even space-as-concept ceases.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","an explicit Vedāntic move — ākāśa is a tanmātra-construct co-arising with śabda; with śabda's cessation, ākāśa (as conception) ceases too. Cf. Vedānta on śabda-tanmātra → ākāśa-bhūta."],["Sivananda","tad viṣṇoḥ paramaṃ padam — the same phrase as Ṛgveda 1.22.20; the verse identifies the laya-goal with the supreme Vaiṣṇava abode."],["Krishnananda","the philosophical insight here is precise — the cessation of even subtle sound is also the cessation of even subtle space; the laya is total."],["Ayyangar","footnotes the Ṛgveda 1.22.20 parallel."],["Deussen","compares Maitri 6.22 — the śabda → ākāśa → mahad-ākāśa progression."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 48","title":"Beyond sound is Brahman-paramātman; mind = nāda; nāda's end = unmanī","sa":["निःशब्दं तत्परं ब्रह्म परमात्मा समीर्यते ।","नादो यावन्मनस्तावन्नादान्तेऽपि मनोन्मनी ॥ ४८ ॥"],"iast":["niḥśabdaṃ tat paraṃ brahma paramātmā samīryate |","nādo yāvan manas tāvan nādānte 'pi manonmanī || 48 ||"],"words":[["niḥ-śabdam tat param brahma","n. nom. + n. acc.","the soundless, that supreme Brahman"],["paramātmā samīryate","m. nom. + 3 sg. pass.","is called the supreme Self"],["nādaḥ yāvat manaḥ tāvat","m. + correl. + n. + correl.","so long there is nāda, so long the mind"],["nāda-ante api manonmanī","m. loc. + emph. + f. nom.","(but) even at the end of nāda, manonmanī"]],"lit":"“The soundless — that supreme Brahman — is called the supreme Self. So long as there is nāda, so long there is mind; (but) even at the end of nāda, (there is) manonmanī (the no-mind state).”","para":"The soundless Brahman is the paramātman. As long as nāda persists, so does mind. When nāda finally ceases — its end is the unmanī, the no-mind state.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","niḥ-śabdam param brahma — Brahman is the soundless. Even the very subtle nāda is on the side of mind; its cessation is unmanī. So nāda-yoga is a self-cancelling path."],["Sivananda","the climax-verse of the practice section — nāda is the door, not the destination."],["Krishnananda","the verse formally identifies the laya-yoga endpoint with Brahman-realisation."],["Ayyangar","renders manonmanī as 'beyond mind'."],["Deussen","compares Maṇḍala-Brāhmaṇa Up. 2.4 for identical phrasing."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 49","title":"Sound dies in the imperishable; vāsanā wanes by nāda-anusandhāna","sa":["सशब्दश्चाक्षरे क्षीणे निःशब्दं परमं पदम् ।","सदा नादानुसन्धानात्सङ्क्षीणा वासना भवेत् ॥ ४९ ॥"],"iast":["saśabdaś cākṣare kṣīṇe niḥśabdaṃ paramaṃ padam |","sadā nādānusandhānāt saṃkṣīṇā vāsanā bhavet || 49 ||"],"words":[["sa-śabdaḥ ca akṣare kṣīṇe","m. nom. + loc. abs.","and the sound-bearing (Oṃ), when extinguished in the imperishable"],["niḥ-śabdam paramam padam","n. nom. + n. nom.","(reveals) the soundless supreme abode"],["sadā nāda-anusandhānāt","adv. + n. abl.","by constant nāda-anusandhāna"],["saṃkṣīṇā vāsanā bhavet","f. nom. pp. + 3 sg. pot.","vāsanā would become wholly attenuated"]],"lit":"“The sound-bearing (Oṃ), when extinguished in the imperishable, (reveals) the soundless supreme abode; by constant nāda-anusandhāna, vāsanā becomes wholly attenuated.”","para":"When even the audible Praṇava dissolves into the akṣara, the soundless supreme abode appears. Sustained nāda-anusandhāna wears out vāsanās completely.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","saśabdaḥ … niḥśabdam — the verse names the central paradox of Section II: sound is used to reach soundlessness. The yogin uses Oṃ's audible form to fade it into its silent core."],["Sivananda","vāsanā-kṣaya is the practical fruit; without it no realisation is stable, even after a vision."],["Krishnananda","saṃkṣīṇā vāsanā — wholly attenuated, not merely subdued. Nāda-anusandhāna is one of the rare practices that wears out latent dispositions."],["Ayyangar","renders sa-śabdaḥ as 'with sound'."],["Deussen","compares Yogasūtra 1.50 on saṃskāra-kṣaya through prajñā."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 50","title":"Mind and prāṇa dissolve in the unstained; crores of nādas and bindus —","sa":["निरञ्जने विलीयेते मनोवायू न संशयः ।","नादकोटिसहस्राणि बिन्दुकोटिशतानि च ॥ ५० ॥"],"iast":["nirañjane vilīyete manovāyū na saṃśayaḥ |","nāda-koṭi-sahasrāṇi bindu-koṭi-śatāni ca || 50 ||"],"words":[["nir-añjane vilīyete","m. loc. + 3 du. pres. pass.","(both) dissolve in the unstained"],["manaḥ-vāyū na saṃśayaḥ","m. nom. du. + neg. + m. nom.","mind-and-prāṇa, (there is) no doubt"],["nāda-koṭi-sahasrāṇi","n. nom. pl.","thousands of crores of nādas"],["bindu-koṭi-śatāni ca","n. nom. pl. + conj.","and hundreds of crores of bindus"]],"lit":"“In the unstained (nirañjana), mind and prāṇa both dissolve — no doubt; (and) thousands of crores of nādas, and hundreds of crores of bindus…” (continues v. 51)","para":"In the unstained (Brahman), both mind and prāṇa dissolve. Even billions of nādas and bindus — the entire experiential proliferation — dissolves with them.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","manas and vāyu (prāṇa) dissolve together — they are the two anchors of jīva-existence; their joint dissolution is the laya-fruit. Nāda-koṭi-sahasrāṇi is hyperbole for the indefinitely many sound-experiences a practitioner accumulates over time."],["Sivananda","an empirical note — practitioners often report a profusion of sub-nādas at advanced stages; this verse assures that all of them go in the final laya."],["Krishnananda","the joint dissolution of manas and vāyu links Nāda-Bindu with Haṭha-Yoga-Pradīpikā, which makes the same claim (the prāṇa stops where the mind stops, and vice versa)."],["Ayyangar","renders nirañjana as 'the stainless'."],["Deussen","compares HYP 4.21–22."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 51","title":"All dissolve in the Brahma-praṇava-nāda; free of states, of all thought","sa":["सर्वे तत्र लयं यान्ति ब्रह्मप्रणवनादके ।","सर्वावस्थाविनिर्मुक्तः सर्वचिन्ताविवर्जितः ॥ ५१ ॥"],"iast":["sarve tatra layaṃ yānti brahma-praṇava-nādake |","sarvāvasthā-vinirmuktaḥ sarva-cintā-vivarjitaḥ || 51 ||"],"words":[["sarve tatra layam yānti","pron. + adv. + m. acc. + 3 pl.","all (those) go there to dissolution"],["brahma-praṇava-nādake","m. loc. sg.","in the Brahma-praṇava-nāda"],["sarva-avasthā-vinirmuktaḥ","m. nom. sg. bahuvr.","freed from all states"],["sarva-cintā-vivarjitaḥ","m. nom. sg. bahuvr.","freed from all thinking"]],"lit":"“All those (nādas and bindus) go there to dissolution — in the Brahma-praṇava-nāda; (the yogin is then) freed from all (avasthā-)states (and) freed from all thinking.”","para":"Every nāda, every bindu dissolves into the Brahma-praṇava-nāda. The yogi becomes free of all states (waking, dream, deep sleep) and of all thought-content.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the avasthā-traya is transcended — the same point made in Amṛta-Bindu v. 11. Section II's nāda-method achieves what Section I's avasthā-analysis described."],["Sivananda","the yogin is now functionally a jīvanmukta — vv. 52–53 describe the outer signs."],["Krishnananda","sarvāvasthā-vinirmukta = transcending the three avasthās, which is turīya; the verse is in clear continuity with Māṇḍūkya 7."],["Ayyangar","renders sarvāvasthā-vinirmukta as 'free from all states of consciousness'."],["Deussen","compares Maitri 6.34 — citta is the substance of all three states, and its dissolution dissolves them."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 52","title":"Like one dead — liberated, no doubt; conch and drum no longer heard","sa":["मृतवत्तिष्ठते योगी स मुक्तो नात्र संशयः ।","शङ्खदुन्दुभिनादं च न शृणोति कदाचन ॥ ५२ ॥"],"iast":["mṛtavat tiṣṭhate yogī sa mukto nātra saṃśayaḥ |","śaṅkha-dundubhi-nādaṃ ca na śṛṇoti kadācana || 52 ||"],"words":[["mṛta-vat tiṣṭhate yogī","adv. + 3 sg. pres. + m. nom.","the yogi stays as if dead"],["saḥ muktaḥ na atra saṃśayaḥ","pron. + m. nom. + neg.","he is liberated — no doubt of it"],["śaṅkha-dundubhi-nādam ca","m. acc. sg. kc.","and (even) the sound of conch and drum"],["na śṛṇoti kadācana","neg. + 3 sg. pres. + indecl.","he never hears"]],"lit":"“The yogi stays as if dead — he is liberated, no doubt of it; he never hears the sound of conch or kettle-drum.”","para":"The yogi remains like one dead — perfectly still. He is liberated, beyond doubt — he no longer perceives even loud sounds like conch or drum.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","mṛtavat — 'like-dead' — is not literal death but the cessation of all outward signs of mental activity; v. 53 will say the body too becomes wood-like."],["Sivananda","the verse describes nirvikalpa samādhi as observed from outside — practical sign of the achieved state."],["Krishnananda","śaṅkha-dundubhi — the loudest of normal sounds — symbolises the strongest sense-stimuli, all now muted in the laya-state."],["Ayyangar","renders mṛtavat as 'corpse-like'."],["Deussen","compares Yoga-Cūḍāmaṇi 36 — same description of the laya-stage."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 53","title":"The body is like wood; cold-heat, pleasure-pain unknown","sa":["काष्ठवज्ज्ञायते देह उन्मन्यावस्थया ध्रुवम् ।","न जानाति स शीतोष्णं न दुःखं न सुखं तथा ॥ ५३ ॥"],"iast":["kāṣṭhavaj jñāyate deha unmanyāvasthayā dhruvam |","na jānāti sa śītoṣṇaṃ na duḥkhaṃ na sukhaṃ tathā || 53 ||"],"words":[["kāṣṭha-vat jñāyate dehaḥ","adv. + 3 sg. pass. + m. nom.","the body is known (= felt) like wood"],["unmanī-avasthayā dhruvam","f. instr. + adv.","by the unmanī-state, surely"],["na jānāti sa śīta-uṣṇam","neg. + 3 sg. + pron. + n. acc.","he does not know cold-or-heat"],["na duḥkham na sukham tathā","neg. + n. acc. + neg. + n. acc. + adv.","nor pain, nor pleasure, likewise"]],"lit":"“By the unmanī-state, the body is surely known as if of wood; he knows neither cold-heat nor pain nor pleasure.”","para":"In the unmanī state, the body feels like a piece of wood — the yogi no longer registers cold/heat or pleasure/pain.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","kāṣṭha-vat — wood-like — is the standard description of advanced samādhi; the body has not died, but its sentience-functions have ceased to report to the mind."],["Sivananda","the dhruvam (surely) is the verse's force — these signs are reliable indicators of the unmanī-state."],["Krishnananda","the verse describes a passing experience, not a permanent state — the next verses will describe the long-term result."],["Ayyangar","renders kāṣṭhavat dehaḥ as 'the body becomes log-like'."],["Deussen","compares HYP 4.110."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 54","title":"Honour and dishonour cast off in samādhi; the three states do not reach the yogi","sa":["न मानं नावमानं च सन्त्यक्त्वा तु समाधिना ।","अवस्थात्रयमन्वेति न चित्तं योगिनः सदा ॥ ५४ ॥"],"iast":["na mānaṃ nāvamānaṃ ca santyaktvā tu samādhinā |","avasthā-trayam anveti na cittaṃ yoginaḥ sadā || 54 ||"],"words":[["na mānam na avamānam ca","neg. + n. acc. + neg. + n. acc. + conj.","neither honour nor dishonour"],["santyaktvā tu samādhinā","ger. + particle + m. instr.","having abandoned (these), through samādhi"],["avasthā-trayam anveti","n. acc. + 3 sg. pres.","(it) follows the three states"],["na cittam yoginaḥ sadā","neg. + n. nom. + m. gen. + adv.","(but) the citta of the yogi never (does)"]],"lit":"“Having abandoned honour and dishonour through samādhi — the three states (waking, dream, deep sleep) never follow the citta of the yogi.”","para":"By means of samādhi, the yogi puts honour and dishonour behind him; from then on, the three avasthās do not 'pursue' (anveti) his citta — i.e., the citta is no longer subject to them.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","anveti is unusual — 'follows after' — used here to mean 'overtake' or 'capture'. The yogi's citta is no longer captured by the three states."],["Sivananda","the verse describes the social-equilibrium of the jīvanmukta — neither praise nor blame moves him."],["Krishnananda","the connection between samādhi (cause) and the three states' non-capture (effect) is the verse's main teaching."],["Ayyangar","footnotes the difficult anveti construction."],["Deussen","compares Bhāg. 11.13.27 for similar avasthā-transcendence."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 55","title":"Free from waking-and-sleep, he attains his own-form-state","sa":["जाग्रन्निद्राविनिर्मुक्तः स्वरूपावस्थतामियात् ॥ ५५ ॥"],"iast":["jāgran-nidrā-vinirmuktaḥ svarūpāvasthatām iyāt || 55 ||"],"words":[["jāgrat-nidrā-vinirmuktaḥ","m. nom. sg. bahuvr.","freed from waking-and-sleep"],["sva-rūpa-avasthatām iyāt","f. acc. + 3 sg. pot.","(he) would attain the state-of-(his)-own-form"]],"lit":"“Freed from waking-and-sleep, he would attain the state of (his) own true form (svarūpāvasthatā).”","para":"Beyond waking and sleeping, he reaches svarūpa-avasthā — the state of being his own (Self-)form.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","svarūpāvasthatā is the precise term: 'standing-in-one's-own-form'. Identical with the yogasūtra 1.3's 'tadā draṣṭuḥ svarūpe avasthānam'."],["Sivananda","the verse is short because the state is beyond elaboration; it is named, not described."],["Krishnananda","the verse pairs jāgrat and nidrā (omitting suṣupti) — likely metri causa; the full three-state transcendence is meant."],["Ayyangar","footnotes the Patañjali parallel."],["Deussen","the verse is metrically irregular (half-śloka); marks a closing stage before the final upajāti."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 56","title":"The perfected yogi — gaze without object, breath without effort, mind without support","sa":["दृष्टिः स्थिरा यस्य विना सदृश्यं","वायुः स्थिरो यस्य विना प्रयत्नम् ।","चित्तं स्थिरं यस्य विनावलम्बं","स ब्रह्मतारान्तरनादरूपः ॥ ५६ ॥"],"iast":["dṛṣṭiḥ sthirā yasya vinā sadṛśyaṃ","vāyuḥ sthiro yasya vinā prayatnam |","cittaṃ sthiraṃ yasya vinā ālambaṃ","sa brahma-tārāntara-nāda-rūpaḥ || 56 ||"],"words":[["dṛṣṭiḥ sthirā yasya","f. nom. + f. nom. + rel. gen.","whose gaze (is) steady"],["vinā sadṛśyam","indecl. + n. acc.","without (any) visible object"],["vāyuḥ sthiraḥ yasya vinā prayatnam","m. nom. + adj. + gen. + indecl. + m. acc.","whose breath (is) steady without effort"],["cittam sthiram yasya vinā ālambam","n. nom. + adj. + gen. + indecl. + m. acc.","whose citta (is) steady without (any) support"],["saḥ brahma-tāra-antara-nāda-rūpaḥ","m. nom. + m. nom. sg. kc.","he (is) the form of the inner Brahma-Tāra-Nāda"]],"lit":"“He whose gaze is steady without (any) visible object, whose breath is steady without effort, whose citta is steady without (any) support — he is the form of the inner Brahma-Tāra-Nāda.”","para":"The closing verse, in upajāti meter. The perfected one is identified by three signs — eyes still without anything to look at, breath still without controlled effort, mind still without any object to hold to. He is the Brahma-Tāra-Nāda (the supreme-Praṇava-Sound) made manifest.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the three lakṣaṇas (gaze, breath, citta — all steady, all without a corresponding 'support') describe natural samādhi (sahaja-samādhi). The closing identification, brahma-tāra-antara-nāda-rūpaḥ, is the Vedānta-rendering of the Vaiṣṇavī-mudrā perfection."],["Sivananda","the most quoted verse from this Upaniṣad in modern hatha-yoga literature; the three signs are the threefold test of perfection."],["Krishnananda","the metrical shift to upajāti marks the verse as a memorial coda; the three 'vinā' (without) negations are the via-negativa lakṣaṇa of the perfected state."],["Ayyangar","the verse is given pride of place; he reads brahma-tāra as 'the Praṇava AUM' specifically."],["Deussen","calls v. 56 the 'crown' of the Upaniṣad and translates with particular care; cites it in the Allgemeine Geschichte as a major Vedānta-locus."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Closing colophon and peace","title":"","sa":["इत्युपनिषत् ॥","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥","इति नादबिन्दूपनिषत्समाप्ता ॥"],"iast":["ity upaniṣat ||  Oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ ||  iti nādabindūpaniṣat samāptā ||"],"words":[],"lit":"[full peace mantra repeated]","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Thus ends the Nādabindu Upaniṣad."]},{"ref":"Thematic Outline","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Verses</td><td>Theme</td><td>Key idea</td></tr><tr><td>Maṅgala</td><td>Vairāja-ātman worship</td><td>The fire of knowledge burns the three karmas.</td></tr><tr><td>1–5</td><td>Oṃ as Haṃsa-bird</td><td>Anatomy of Oṃ: A right wing, U left, M tail, ardha-mātrā head; loka-body map; Haṃsa-yogi not destroyed by sin.</td></tr><tr><td>6–8</td><td>The Four Mātrās</td><td>Āgneyī, Vāyavī, Bhānumaṇḍala-saṅkāśā, Vāruṇī (ardha-mātrā). 4 mātrās × 3 svaras → 12 kalās.</td></tr><tr><td>9–11</td><td>The Twelve Kalās</td><td>Ghoṣiṇī, Vidyunmātrā, Pataṅginī, Vāyuveginī, Nāmadheyā, Aindrī, Vaiṣṇavī, Śāṅkarī, Mahatī, Dhṛti, Nārī, Brāhmī.</td></tr><tr><td>12–16</td><td>Death in each kalā</td><td>Ascending fruits: Bhārata-cakravartin → Yakṣa → Vidyādhara → Gandharva → Soma-loka → Indra-sāyujya → Viṣṇu-pada → Rudra-loka → Mahar → Janar → Tapar → Brahman.</td></tr><tr><td>17–20</td><td>The Supreme beyond all kalās</td><td>Pure, all-pervading, stainless, auspicious; mind-laya; bonds dissolved; paramānanda.</td></tr><tr><td>21–29</td><td>Advaita interlude on prārabdha</td><td>Rope-snake simile; body is superimposed; 'prārabdha' is taught for the unawakened only.</td></tr><tr><td>30–32</td><td>Praṇava → Nāda emerges</td><td>Brahma-praṇava-sandhāna; Self manifests like sun on cloud-clearing; siddhāsana + Vaiṣṇavī-mudrā + right ear; nāda covers outer sound; conquering pakṣa-vipakṣa → Turīya.</td></tr><tr><td>33–35</td><td>The Ten Inner Sounds</td><td>Beginning: ocean–cloud–kettledrum–waterfall. Middle: mardala–bell–trumpet. End: kiṅkiṇī–flute–vīṇā–bhramara.</td></tr><tr><td>36–41</td><td>Gross to subtle</td><td>Always grasp the subtler; oscillate freely within nāda; first hook is the path; drop thought and effort; citta dissolves in nāda.</td></tr><tr><td>42–45</td><td>Bee-and-honey bestiary</td><td>Citta is bee → honey of nāda; serpent of mind charmed; elephant of mind goaded; deer of mind snared.</td></tr><tr><td>46–49</td><td>Mano-laya in nāda</td><td>Inner ocean's shore; mind dissolves in Viṣṇu's paramaṃ padam; tāvad ākāśa-saṅkalpaḥ yāvac chabdaḥ; soundless Brahman; vāsanā-kṣaya.</td></tr><tr><td>50–53</td><td>All dissolves; jīvanmukti signs</td><td>Crores of nādas/bindus dissolve; mind-prāṇa dissolve in nirañjana; yogi like a corpse; body like wood; no cold/heat/pain/pleasure.</td></tr><tr><td>54–55</td><td>Three avasthās do not reach</td><td>Honour-dishonour gone in samādhi; jāgrat-nidrā-vinirmukta; svarūpa-avasthatā.</td></tr><tr><td>56</td><td>The three signs of perfection</td><td>Gaze without object, breath without effort, mind without support — Brahma-Tāra-Nāda-Rūpa.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Reading List","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Primary text: sanskritdocuments.org, nadabindu.itx (Devanāgarī + ITRANS).","Classical Sanskrit commentary: Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin's bhāṣya, in A. Mahadeva Sastri (ed.), The Yoga Upaniṣads, Adyar Library, 1920 — with the Sanskrit commentary.","English translation + commentary: T.R.S. Ayyangar, The Yoga Upaniṣads, Adyar Library, 1938 (reprint Adyar 1952) — single most useful one-volume reference for the 20 Yoga Upaniṣads.","Early English translation: K. Nārāyaṇaswāmi Aiyar, Thirty Minor Upanishads, 1914 — straightforward, widely-available rendering.","Practical / meditative: Swami Sivananda, Music as Yoga, Kundalini Yoga, and the nāda-chapter of Yoga in Daily Life (Divine Life Society) — uses Nāda Bindu Section II as the foundational manual.","Philosophical: Swami Krishnananda, audio lecture series on the Yoga Upaniṣads (Divine Life Society archive).","Indological / textual: Paul Deussen, Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda (1897); English translation by V.M. Bedekar &amp; G.B. Palsule, Sixty Upaniṣads of the Veda, Motilal Banarsidass, 1980, vol. 2.","Receiving tradition: Haṭha-Yoga-Pradīpikā 4.65–102 — quotes / paraphrases Nāda Bindu Section II extensively and is the principal medieval reception of this text."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Closing Note","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["All Sanskrit follows the 56-verse Ṛgveda recension (Ayyangar / sanskritdocuments.org). Where MS variants are known they are flagged in-line. IAST transliteration is given for every verse; word-by-word gloss includes grammatical notation (gender, case, number; finite-verb person/tense).","Commentary summaries are digests in this compiler's words, drawing on the cited published works; they are not direct translations of the commentators. For citation-grade text, consult the primary editions listed above.","Companion volume in the Bindu-Upaniṣad series: Amṛta_Bindu_Upanishad.docx (22 verses)."],"kind":"note"}]}]};
